// Modern India (1498 Europeans -> post-1947) — group 'his-mod'. Coordinates pin the actual site/town.
GEO.add({
 id:'his-europeans', group:'his-mod', title:'European Trading Settlements & Forts', bn:'ইউরোপীয় বাণিজ্যকুঠি ও দুর্গ', icon:'⛵', world:false,
 intro:'পর্তুগিজ (১৪৯৮) → ডাচ (১৬০৫) → ইংরেজ (১৬১৩) → ড্যানিশ (১৬২০) → ফরাসি (১৬৬৮) — কে কোথায় প্রথম কুঠি/দুর্গ বানাল, রঙ দেখে মিলিয়ে নাও।',
 types:{portuguese:['Portuguese','#2e7d32'], dutch:['Dutch','#ef6c00'], english:['English / British','#c62828'], french:['French','#1565c0'], danish:['Danish','#6a1b9a']},
 pts:[
  ['Kappad',11.385,75.718,'Kerala (Kozhikode)','Vasco da Gama landed here 20 May 1498 — sea route to India via Cape of Good Hope.','portuguese',
    { old: "Kappakadavu (local name of the beach)", more: "Vasco da Gama's fleet reached the Malabar coast near Kappad on 20 May 1498 after rounding the Cape of Good Hope, opening the direct sea route from Europe to India. Kappad lay in the Zamorin of Calicut's territory. A memorial stone marks the landing. The voyage began the Portuguese phase of European commerce and naval power in the Indian Ocean." } ],
  ['Calicut (Kozhikode)',11.249,75.784,'Kerala','Zamorin\'s port; Cabral set up Portuguese factory 1500; English factory 1615 (Keeling).','portuguese',
    { old: "Calicut (European name) → Kozhikode (official)", more: "Capital of the Zamorin (Samoothiri), Calicut was the leading pepper port of Malabar. Pedro Alvares Cabral set up a Portuguese factory in 1500, which was attacked by Arab merchants, leading to Portuguese bombardment of the town. The English factory came in 1615 under Captain William Keeling. The word \"calico\" derives from Calicut. Ceded to the British by Tipu in 1792 (Treaty of Seringapatam)." } ],
  ['Kannur — St Angelo Fort',11.854,75.372,'Kerala','Built 1505 by Francisco de Almeida, first Portuguese viceroy; Dutch 1663; British 1790.','portuguese',
    { old: "Cannanore (colonial name of Kannur); Fortaleza de Santo Angelo", more: "Built in 1505 by Dom Francisco de Almeida, the first Portuguese Viceroy of India, with the Kolathiri Raja's consent. The Dutch captured it in 1663 and sold it to the Arakkal family (Ali Rajas) in 1772; the British took it in 1790 and made it a major military station on the Malabar coast. Now an ASI-protected monument." } ],
  ['Kochi (Fort Kochi)',9.966,76.242,'Kerala','Fort Manuel 1503 — first European fort in India; Vasco da Gama died 1524, buried St Francis Church; Dutch 1663.','portuguese',
    { old: "Cochin; Fort Manuel (Portuguese); Kochi (official name since 1996)", more: "The Raja of Cochin, hostile to the Zamorin, allowed the Portuguese to build Fort Manuel (1503), the first European fort in India. Vasco da Gama died in Kochi in 1524 and was first buried in St Francis Church (remains later taken to Portugal). The Dutch captured Cochin in 1663 and renovated Mattancherry Palace (the \"Dutch Palace\"); the British took over in 1795." } ],
  ['Kollam — Tangasseri (Quilon)',8.884,76.564,'Kerala','Portuguese factory 1502, Fort St Thomas 1518; taken by Dutch 1661.','portuguese',
    { old: "Quilon (colonial name of Kollam); Desinganadu; Fort Thomas (Portuguese)", more: "Kollam was an ancient port known to Chinese and Arab traders (the Kollam era/Kollavarsham began 825 CE). The Portuguese opened a factory in 1502 and built Fort St Thomas at Tangasseri in 1518. The Dutch took it in 1661; Tangasseri later remained a tiny British enclave attached to Madras Presidency until 1950. Fort ruins and a lighthouse survive." } ],
  ['Kodungallur (Cranganore)',10.234,76.195,'Kerala (Thrissur)','Portuguese fort at Kottappuram (c. 1536); taken by Dutch 1662.','portuguese',
    { old: "Mahodayapuram / Makotai (Chera capital); Cranganore (colonial); linked with ancient Muziris", more: "Kodungallur was the capital of the later Cheras (Mahodayapuram) and is linked with the ancient Indo-Roman port of Muziris (excavations at nearby Pattanam). Tradition holds St Thomas landed here in 52 CE, and the Cheraman Juma Masjid is traditionally among India's oldest mosques. The Portuguese built a fort at Kottappuram c. 1536; the Dutch took it in 1662." } ],
  ['Old Goa',15.503,73.912,'Goa','Albuquerque captured Goa from Bijapur 1510; capital of Estado da India; Bom Jesus (St Francis Xavier).','portuguese',
    { old: "Velha Goa; Ela (Adil Shahi city); \"Goa Dourada\" (Golden Goa)", more: "Afonso de Albuquerque captured Goa from the Adil Shahi Sultanate of Bijapur in 1510; it became the capital of the Portuguese Estado da India. Its churches and convents, including the Basilica of Bom Jesus (relics of St Francis Xavier) and Se Cathedral, are a UNESCO World Heritage Site (1986). Epidemics forced the capital to shift to Panaji (Nova Goa), officially in 1843." } ],
  ['Chaul (Revdanda)',18.546,72.927,'Maharashtra (Raigad)','Portuguese fort; Battle of Chaul 1508 — Mamluk–Gujarat fleet killed Lourenco de Almeida; Korlai fort opposite.','portuguese',
    { old: "Chaul / Cheul (Portuguese Chaul); Simylla (Greek sources, attributed)", more: "Chaul was a major port of the Ahmadnagar Sultanate. In the Battle of Chaul (1508) a Mamluk-Gujarat fleet defeated the Portuguese, killing Lourenco de Almeida, son of Viceroy Francisco de Almeida, who avenged him at Diu in 1509. The Portuguese fortified Chaul (Revdanda) and Korlai across the creek. The Marathas took the area in 1739-40." } ],
  ['Vasai (Bassein) Fort',19.330,72.815,'Maharashtra (Palghar)','Ceded by Bahadur Shah of Gujarat, Treaty of Bassein 1534; captured by Marathas (Chimaji Appa) 1739.','portuguese',
    { old: "Bassein (Portuguese Baçaim); Fort of St Sebastian", more: "Sultan Bahadur Shah of Gujarat ceded Bassein to the Portuguese by the Treaty of Bassein (1534). It became the Portuguese \"Court of the North\". Chimaji Appa, brother of Peshwa Bajirao I, captured it in May 1739 after a long siege. In 1802 Peshwa Baji Rao II signed the Treaty of Bassein accepting Wellesley's Subsidiary Alliance here." } ],
  ['Daman — Moti Daman Fort',20.410,72.833,'Dadra & NH and Daman & Diu','Portuguese from 1559 to Dec 1961 (Operation Vijay).','portuguese',
    { old: "Damão (Portuguese); Nani Daman & Moti Daman", more: "The Portuguese seized Daman from the Gujarat Sultanate in 1559 and fortified Moti Daman, with the Bom Jesus church inside. It remained Portuguese until India's Operation Vijay in December 1961, along with Goa and Diu. It was part of Goa, Daman and Diu UT till 1987 and merged with Dadra and Nagar Haveli into one UT in 2020." } ],
  ['Diu Fortress',20.715,70.996,'Dadra & NH and Daman & Diu','Fort 1535 (treaty with Bahadur Shah); naval Battle of Diu 1509 — Almeida defeated Mamluk–Gujarat fleet.','portuguese',
    { old: "Dio (Portuguese); Fortaleza de Diu", more: "In the naval Battle of Diu (Feb 1509) Francisco de Almeida defeated a combined Mamluk, Gujarat and Calicut fleet, giving the Portuguese mastery of the Arabian Sea. Bahadur Shah of Gujarat allowed a fort in 1535 while seeking help against Humayun, and was killed in 1537 during a meeting on a Portuguese ship. Sieges of 1538 and 1546 failed. Liberated December 1961." } ],
  ['Hooghly–Bandel',22.919,88.395,'West Bengal (Hooghly)','Portuguese settlement 1579 (Akbar\'s farman); Bandel Basilica 1599; sacked by Shah Jahan 1632; English factory 1651.','portuguese',
    { old: "Hooghly / Ugolim (Portuguese); Bandel from Persian \"bandar\" (port)", more: "Portuguese traders settled at Hooghly c. 1579 under a farman of Akbar; the Bandel Church (Basilica of the Holy Rosary, founded 1599) is among the oldest churches in Bengal. Shah Jahan's forces under Qasim Khan sacked Portuguese Hooghly in 1632 over piracy and slave-raiding. The English set up a factory at Hooghly in 1651." } ],
  ['Santhome (Mylapore)',13.034,80.278,'Tamil Nadu (Chennai)','Portuguese Sao Tome de Meliapor 1523; St Thomas Basilica over the apostle\'s reputed tomb.','portuguese',
    { old: "São Tomé de Meliapor (Portuguese); Mylapore (Mayilai)", more: "The Portuguese built Sao Tome (1523) around the reputed tomb of the Apostle Thomas, said to have been martyred in 72 CE. The neo-Gothic San Thome Basilica (rebuilt 1893) stands over the tomb. Mylapore is also known for the Kapaleeshwarar temple. The settlement passed through Golconda, French and finally English (1749) hands." } ],
  ['Pipli',21.564,87.336,'Odisha (Balasore)','Portuguese trading post near Subarnarekha mouth (16th c.); later Dutch factory.','portuguese',
    { old: "Pipli / Pipley on the Subarnarekha", more: "A port near the mouth of the Subarnarekha, Pipli was used by the Portuguese in the 16th century and later by the Dutch and English as a trading station in Mughal Orissa, also notorious as a slave market. Silting of the river led to its decline in favour of Balasore by the late 17th century." } ],
  ['Masulipatnam (Machilipatnam)',16.170,81.130,'Andhra Pradesh (Krishna)','First Dutch factory in India 1605; English 1611 (Golconda\'s port); French 1669.','dutch',
    { old: "Masulipatnam / Masulipatam (colonial); Bandar (Qutb Shahi port name); Maisolos (Ptolemy, attributed)", more: "Principal port of the Qutb Shahi kingdom of Golconda and a centre of kalamkari and fine textiles. The Dutch founded their first factory in India here in 1605; the English came in 1611 (ship Globe) and the French in 1669. Colonel Forde captured it from the French in 1759 and Salabat Jang ceded the surrounding Northern Circars area to the English." } ],
  ['Pulicat — Fort Geldria',13.418,80.319,'Tamil Nadu (Tiruvallur)','Dutch from 1610; Fort Geldria 1613; Dutch Coromandel HQ until 1690.','dutch',
    { old: "Pazhaverkadu (Tamil); Fort Geldria (Dutch)", more: "The Dutch set up a factory at Pulicat in 1610 under an agreement with Vijayanagara ruler Venkata II and built Fort Geldria in 1613. It was Dutch Coromandel headquarters until 1690, when HQ moved to Nagapattinam. A Dutch cemetery survives. Pulicat Lake is India's second-largest brackish-water lagoon after Chilika." } ],
  ['Sadras (Sadurangapattinam)',12.525,80.162,'Tamil Nadu (Chengalpattu)','Dutch fort and fine-cotton textile factory (17th c.).','dutch',
    { old: "Sadras (Dutch/English)", more: "The Dutch VOC set up a trading post at Sadras in the mid-17th century, known for fine muslin and cotton textiles; the small fort and Dutch cemetery are ASI-protected. It changed hands between the Dutch and British several times and was finally handed to the British in 1825 under the Anglo-Dutch Treaty of 1824." } ],
  ['Nagapattinam',10.767,79.845,'Tamil Nadu','Portuguese 1554; Dutch 1658 — Dutch Coromandel HQ 1690–1781; British 1781.','dutch',
    { old: "Negapatam (colonial); Nagapattinam", more: "An ancient Chola port associated with Buddhist viharas, including the Chudamani Vihara built by the Srivijaya king Sri Mara Vijayottunga under Rajaraja I (c. 1006). The Portuguese settled here in 1554; the Dutch took it in 1658 and made it their Coromandel capital (1690-1781). The British captured it in 1781 during the Fourth Anglo-Dutch War." } ],
  ['Bheemunipatnam (Bimlipatam)',17.885,83.439,'Andhra Pradesh (Visakhapatnam)','Dutch VOC factory 17th c.; Dutch cemetery; oldest municipality of Andhra (1861).','dutch',
    { old: "Bimlipatam / Bimlipatnam (colonial)", more: "The Dutch VOC established a factory at Bimlipatam in the 17th century for textiles and rice; a Dutch fort ruin and cemetery survive. It was attacked by the Marathas in 1754 and taken by the British in 1781 and again in 1795. Constituted a municipality in 1861, among the oldest in Andhra Pradesh." } ],
  ['Chinsurah (Chuchura)',22.887,88.397,'West Bengal (Hooghly)','Dutch factory 1653 (Fort Gustavus); Battle of Biderra 1759; handed to British 1825 for Bencoolen.','dutch',
    { old: "Chinsura; Chuchura; Fort Gustavus (Dutch)", more: "The Dutch built a factory at Chinsurah in 1653 and later Fort Gustavus, making it the centre of their Bengal trade. At the Battle of Chinsurah/Biderra (Nov 1759) the English under Colonel Forde defeated the Dutch, ending their ambitions in Bengal. By the Anglo-Dutch Treaty of 1824 Chinsurah was exchanged for Bencoolen in Sumatra (effective 1825)." } ],
  ['Thoothukudi (Tuticorin)',8.806,78.145,'Tamil Nadu','Portuguese pearl-fishery base (Paravars); Dutch 1658; British 1825.','dutch',
    { old: "Tuticorin (colonial); Thoothukudi", more: "Centre of the Gulf of Mannar pearl fishery worked by the Parava community, who converted to Catholicism under Portuguese patronage and St Francis Xavier in the 1530s-40s. The Dutch took Tuticorin in 1658 and the British in 1825. V.O. Chidambaram Pillai founded the Swadeshi Steam Navigation Company here in 1906." } ],
  ['Colachel (1741)',8.179,77.256,'Tamil Nadu (Kanyakumari)','Marthanda Varma of Travancore defeated Dutch, 10 Aug 1741; De Lannoy captured — ended Dutch power in Kerala.','dutch',
    { old: "Kulachal (Colachel)", more: "On 10 August 1741 Marthanda Varma of Travancore defeated the Dutch East India Company force at Colachel, a rare defeat of a European power by an Indian kingdom. The captured Dutch commander Eustachius De Lannoy entered Travancore service and modernised its army (Udayagiri fort). The Treaty of Mavelikkara (1753) ended Dutch political power in Kerala." } ],
  ['Surat',21.205,72.840,'Gujarat','First English factory 1613 (Jahangir\'s farman); Thomas Roe 1615; Dutch 1616; first French factory 1668 (Caron).','english',
    { old: "Suryapur (traditional); Bandar-i-Mubarak / Bab-ul-Makka (Mughal Hajj port)", more: "Chief Mughal port and gateway for Hajj pilgrims. The English set up their first permanent factory at Surat in 1613 under Jahangir's farman, after Captain Best's victory over the Portuguese at Swally (1612). Sir Thomas Roe reached Jahangir's court in 1615. The Dutch (1616) and French (1668, Francois Caron) followed. Shivaji sacked Surat in 1664 and 1670." } ],
  ['Armagaon (Durgarajupatnam)',13.983,80.116,'Andhra Pradesh (Tirupati)','English factory & fort 1626, abandoned 1641 for Madras; c. 36 miles north of Pulicat.','english',
    { old: "Armagaon (English)", more: "The English East India Company built a factory with a small fort at Armagaon in 1626, as a Coromandel base for textiles. It proved commercially poor and was abandoned in 1641 when Francis Day and Andrew Cogan moved the establishment south to Madraspatnam, where Fort St George was being built." } ],
  ['Chennai — Fort St George',13.080,80.287,'Tamil Nadu','Land granted 1639 to Francis Day; fort 1644 — first English fortress in India.','english',
    { old: "Madraspatnam / Chennapatnam → Madras → Chennai (1996)", more: "In 1639 Francis Day and Andrew Cogan obtained land from Damarla Venkatadri Nayaka, a vassal of the Vijayanagara ruler at Chandragiri. The fort, completed c. 1644, was the first English fortified settlement in India. It now houses the Tamil Nadu Legislative Assembly and Secretariat; St Mary's Church (1680) inside is the oldest Anglican church east of Suez." } ],
  ['Mumbai — Bombay Castle',18.932,72.838,'Maharashtra','Portuguese dowry to Charles II 1661; leased to EIC 1668 at £10/yr; EIC western HQ from 1687.','english',
    { old: "Bom Baia (Portuguese); Bombay → Mumbai (1995)", more: "The seven islands of Bombay were part of Catherine of Braganza's dowry to Charles II (1661). The Crown leased them to the EIC in 1668 for £10 a year. Governor Gerald Aungier developed the town; in 1687 the EIC shifted its western headquarters from Surat to Bombay. Bombay Castle, a fortified Portuguese manor house, was the EIC's administrative core." } ],
  ['Kolkata — old Fort William',22.573,88.350,'West Bengal','Job Charnock at Sutanuti 1690; Sutanuti, Gobindapur, Kalikata bought 1698; Black Hole 1756; new fort 1781.','english',
    { old: "Kalikata (with Sutanuti, Gobindapur) → Calcutta → Kolkata (2001)", more: "Job Charnock settled at Sutanuti in 1690; zamindari rights over Sutanuti, Gobindapur and Kalikata were bought in 1698 from the Sabarna Roy Choudhury family. The old Fort William (c. 1696-1706, named after William III) fell to Siraj-ud-Daula in 1756 (Black Hole incident). The new Fort William on the Maidan was completed in 1781. Calcutta was capital of British India till 1911." } ],
  ['Kasimbazar',24.120,88.280,'West Bengal (Murshidabad)','English silk factory 1658; Dutch & French factories too; Warren Hastings served here.','english',
    { old: "Cossimbazar / Kasimbazar", more: "A thriving silk centre near Murshidabad, the Nawabi capital of Bengal. The English set up a factory in 1658, and the Dutch, French and Armenians also traded here. Warren Hastings served at the Kasimbazar factory early in his career. Siraj-ud-Daula seized the English factory in June 1756 before attacking Calcutta." } ],
  ['Balasore',21.497,86.923,'Odisha','English factory c. 1640s; Dutch, Danish & French factories as well.','english',
    { old: "Baleshwar; Balasore (colonial)", more: "The English set up a factory at Balasore in the 1630s-40s (after Hariharpur, 1633), an important base before they settled at Hugli and Calcutta. The Dutch, Danes and French also had factories here. Today it is known for the Chandipur missile test range (Integrated Test Range) nearby." } ],
  ['Thalassery (Tellicherry) Fort',11.748,75.486,'Kerala (Kannur)','English pepper factory 1683; fort 1708 — base for Malabar trade.','english',
    { old: "Tellicherry (colonial)", more: "The English East India Company set up a pepper and cardamom factory at Tellicherry in 1683 and fortified it in 1708. It was the main British base on the Malabar coast during the Mysore wars. Thalassery is also associated with Hermann Gundert (Malayalam grammar and dictionary), the early Malayalam newspaper Rajyasamacharam (1847), and the arrival of cricket and circus in Kerala." } ],
  ['Anjengo (Anchuthengu) Fort',8.663,76.764,'Kerala (Thiruvananthapuram)','English fort 1695 with Attingal Rani\'s permission; Attingal revolt 1721 against the English.','english',
    { old: "Anjengo (English); Anchuthengu", more: "The Rani of Attingal permitted the English East India Company to build a fort at Anjengo in the 1690s. In 1721 local people, angered by EIC trading abuses, massacred an English party going to Attingal - the \"Attingal Outbreak\", often cited as the first organised revolt against the English in India. Historian Robert Orme was born here." } ],
  ['Fort St David (Cuddalore)',11.740,79.781,'Tamil Nadu','Bought 1690; EIC southern HQ 1746–52 when Madras fell to French; Clive governor 1756.','english',
    { old: "Tegnapatam / Devanampattinam (Cuddalore)", more: "The English bought Devanampattinam in 1690 from the Maratha ruler of Gingee and named the fort Fort St David. It was EIC headquarters on the Coromandel coast from 1746 to 1752 after Madras fell to the French under La Bourdonnais. Robert Clive was appointed its governor in 1756. The French under Lally captured and demolished it in 1758." } ],
  ['Puducherry',11.934,79.829,'Puducherry','French 1674 (Francois Martin); HQ of French India; Dupleix governor 1742–54.','french',
    { old: "Pondicherry → Puducherry (2006); Poduke (Periplus, attributed to nearby Arikamedu)", more: "Francois Martin founded the French settlement in 1674 on land granted by the Bijapur Sultanate's governor. It became capital of French India. Under Governor-General Dupleix (1742-54) the French played kingmaker in the Carnatic Wars. Captured by the British several times and restored, it was transferred de facto to India in 1954 (de jure 1962). The Indo-Roman site Arikamedu lies nearby." } ],
  ['Chandernagore (Chandannagar)',22.857,88.370,'West Bengal (Hooghly)','French 1673/1688; Dupleix intendant 1731–41; captured by Clive & Watson 1757.','french',
    { old: "Chandernagore (French); Farasdanga", more: "The French established a factory here in 1673 (permanent from 1688). Dupleix was its intendant from 1731 to 1741 and made it prosperous. Clive and Admiral Watson captured it in March 1757, shortly before Plassey. Restored to France, it voted in a 1949 plebiscite to join India, was transferred de facto in 1950 and merged into West Bengal in 1955." } ],
  ['Mahe',11.701,75.537,'Puducherry (Kerala coast)','French 1721–25; named after Mahe de La Bourdonnais.','french',
    { old: "Mayyazhi (Malayalam); Mahé (French)", more: "The French acquired Mayyazhi from the local ruler c. 1721-25 and renamed it after Bertrand-Francois Mahe de La Bourdonnais, who helped capture it. It was a French pepper-trade enclave in Malabar. The Mahe liberation movement, led by I.K. Kumaran, culminated in transfer to India in 1954; it is now part of Puducherry UT." } ],
  ['Yanam',16.733,82.217,'Puducherry (Andhra coast)','French settlement 1731 on the Godavari delta.','french',
    { old: "Yanaon (French)", more: "The French established a settlement at Yanam on the Godavari delta in 1731 (a factory existed from c. 1723), trading in textiles. It changed hands with the British during the 18th-century wars. After a pro-merger uprising in 1954 it was transferred to India with the other French enclaves; it is now a district of Puducherry UT surrounded by Andhra Pradesh." } ],
  ['Karaikal',10.933,79.832,'Puducherry (Tamil Nadu coast)','French 1739, acquired from Raja of Tanjore.','french',
    { old: "Karikal (French)", more: "The French acquired Karaikal from the Maratha Raja of Tanjore in 1739. It was the second most important French settlement on the Coromandel coast after Pondicherry. Transferred de facto to India in 1954 and now a district of Puducherry UT; the Thirunallar Saneeswaran temple lies nearby." } ],
  ['Tranquebar — Fort Dansborg',11.025,79.856,'Tamil Nadu (Mayiladuthurai)','Danish 1620 (Ove Gjedde, Thanjavur Nayak); Ziegenbalg\'s mission 1706; sold to British 1845.','danish',
    { old: "Tharangambadi (\"land of the singing waves\"); Tranquebar (Danish)", more: "Danish admiral Ove Gjedde obtained Tranquebar from the Thanjavur Nayak Raghunatha in 1620 and built Fort Dansborg. In 1706 the German Lutheran missionaries Bartholomaeus Ziegenbalg and Heinrich Plutschau began the first Protestant mission in India here and printed the Tamil New Testament (1715). Denmark sold Tranquebar to the British in 1845." } ],
  ['Serampore',22.750,88.340,'West Bengal (Hooghly)','Danish Frederiksnagore 1755; Carey–Marshman–Ward Baptist mission 1800; sold to British 1845.','danish',
    { old: "Frederiksnagore (Danish); Srirampur", more: "The Danish East India Company acquired Serampore in 1755 and named it Frederiksnagore. The Baptist \"Serampore trio\" - William Carey, Joshua Marshman and William Ward - set up a mission and printing press (1800), printed Bible translations in many Indian languages, published Digdarshan and Samachar Darpan (1818) and founded Serampore College (1818). Sold to the British in 1845." } ],
  ['Nicobar (Nancowry)',7.980,93.550,'Andaman & Nicobar','Danish colony "Frederiksoerne" from 1756; sold to Britain 1868.','danish',
    { old: "Frederiksøerne / Ny Danmark (Danish names for the Nicobars)", more: "Denmark claimed the Nicobar Islands from 1756 as \"Frederiksoerne\", attempting settlements and Moravian missions on Nancowry and nearby islands, which failed largely due to malaria. Denmark sold its rights to Britain in 1868 and the islands came under the Andaman administration at Port Blair. Nancowry was badly hit by the 2004 tsunami." } ]
 ]
});

GEO.add({
 id:'his-british', group:'his-mod', title:'British Expansion: Battles & Treaties 1757–1857', bn:'ব্রিটিশ সম্প্রসারণ: যুদ্ধ ও সন্ধি', icon:'⚔', world:false,
 intro:'কর্নাটক → বাংলা → মহীশূর → মারাঠা → শিখ → স্বত্ববিলোপ নীতি — যুদ্ধ, সন্ধি আর অধিগ্রহণ আলাদা রঙে সাল ধরে পড়ো।',
 types:{battle:['Battle / siege','#c62828'], treaty:['Treaty / subsidiary alliance','#1565c0'], 'annexation-capital':['Annexed state / capital','#6a1b9a']},
 pts:[
  ['Adyar (1746)',13.006,80.257,'Tamil Nadu (Chennai)','First Carnatic War — small French force defeated Nawab Anwaruddin\'s army; proved trained infantry\'s power.','battle',
    { old: "Battle of St Thomé / Mylapore (1746)", more: "Fought on 24 October 1746 during the First Carnatic War, after the French under La Bourdonnais had captured Madras. A small French force under Captain Paradis routed the much larger army of Mahfuz Khan, son of Nawab Anwaruddin of the Carnatic, at the Adyar river. It showed that small, disciplined European-trained infantry could defeat large Indian armies, encouraging Dupleix's political ambitions." } ],
  ['Ambur (1749)',12.780,78.718,'Tamil Nadu (Tirupattur)','Second Carnatic War opens; Chanda Sahib & Muzaffar Jang (French-backed) killed Anwaruddin.','battle',
    { old: "Ambur (Arcot district, colonial)", more: "On 3 August 1749 Chanda Sahib and Muzaffar Jang, backed by Dupleix's French troops, defeated and killed Nawab Anwaruddin of the Carnatic. Chanda Sahib became Nawab of Arcot. This opened the Second Carnatic War (1749-54), in which the English backed Anwaruddin's son Muhammad Ali against the French-backed claimants." } ],
  ['Arcot (1751)',12.905,79.324,'Tamil Nadu (Ranipet)','Clive seized and held Arcot for 50 days — turning point of Second Carnatic War.','battle',
    { old: "Arcot (Arkadu); capital of the Nawabs of the Carnatic", more: "In September 1751 Robert Clive, with about 500 men, seized Arcot, capital of Chanda Sahib, to relieve pressure on Muhammad Ali besieged at Trichinopoly. He held the fort for about 50 days against a much larger force under Raza Sahib. The siege made Clive's reputation and turned the Second Carnatic War in the English favour; the Treaty of Pondicherry (1754) followed Dupleix's recall." } ],
  ['Plassey (1757)',23.800,88.250,'West Bengal (Nadia)','23 June 1757: Clive defeated Siraj-ud-Daula; Mir Jafar\'s betrayal — British political power in Bengal began.','battle',
    { old: "Palashi (from palash trees); Plassey (anglicised)", more: "On 23 June 1757 Robert Clive defeated Nawab Siraj-ud-Daula of Bengal near Palashi on the Bhagirathi. Mir Jafar, Siraj's commander, stood aside under a secret pact with Clive, with Jagat Seth, Omichand and Rai Durlabh involved. Mir Jafar was made Nawab and Siraj was killed. Plassey marked the beginning of British political power in India and the drain of Bengal's wealth." } ],
  ['Wandiwash (1760)',12.500,79.620,'Tamil Nadu (Vandavasi)','Third Carnatic War: Eyre Coote defeated Lally; ended French ambitions in India.','battle',
    { old: "Vandavasi (Tamil); Wandiwash (colonial)", more: "On 22 January 1760, in the Third Carnatic War (part of the Seven Years' War), Sir Eyre Coote defeated the French under Comte de Lally; Bussy was captured. Pondicherry fell in January 1761. Though restored by the Treaty of Paris (1763), the French were reduced to traders, leaving the English without a European rival in India." } ],
  ['Buxar (1764)',25.567,83.983,'Bihar','Hector Munro defeated Mir Qasim, Shuja-ud-Daula & Shah Alam II; led to Diwani of Bengal.','battle',
    { old: "Baksar (Bhojpuri/Hindi); Buxar (colonial)", more: "On 22/23 October 1764 Major Hector Munro defeated the combined armies of Mir Qasim (deposed Nawab of Bengal), Shuja-ud-Daula of Awadh and the Mughal emperor Shah Alam II. Unlike Plassey, it was a decisive military victory. It led to the Treaty of Allahabad (1765) and confirmed the EIC as the paramount power in eastern India." } ],
  ['Allahabad (Treaty 1765)',25.430,81.877,'Uttar Pradesh (Prayagraj)','Clive got Diwani of Bengal, Bihar, Orissa from Shah Alam II; Awadh restored to Shuja; Dual Government.','treaty',
    { old: "Prayag; Ilahabad/Allahabad (Akbar, 1575) → Prayagraj (2018)", more: "Signed on 12 and 16 August 1765 by Robert Clive. Shah Alam II granted the EIC the Diwani (revenue rights) of Bengal, Bihar and Orissa for an annual tribute of Rs 26 lakh and received Kora and Allahabad. Shuja-ud-Daula got Awadh back on paying Rs 50 lakh. The result was the \"Dual Government\" of Bengal (1765-72), abolished by Warren Hastings." } ],
  ['Purandar (Treaty 1776)',18.283,73.983,'Maharashtra (Pune)','First Anglo-Maratha War: Calcutta council\'s treaty with Nana Phadnavis, annulling Treaty of Surat.','treaty',
    { old: "Purandar fort (Purandhar), near Saswad", more: "Signed on 1 March 1776 between the Calcutta Supreme Council (under Warren Hastings) and the Maratha regency council of Nana Phadnavis during the First Anglo-Maratha War. It annulled the Bombay government's Treaty of Surat (1775) with Raghunathrao; the British kept Salsette. It was never fully implemented and war resumed. Purandar is also the site of the 1665 Shivaji-Jai Singh treaty." } ],
  ['Wadgaon (1779)',18.749,73.641,'Maharashtra (Pune)','British army surrendered to Marathas — humiliating Convention of Wadgaon.','battle',
    { old: "Vadgaon Maval (Talegaon area); Convention of Wargaum", more: "In January 1779 a Bombay army marching on Pune to install Raghunathrao was surrounded by the Marathas under Mahadji Scindia and Tukoji Holkar near Talegaon. It surrendered at Wadgaon and signed a humiliating convention giving up all gains since 1773. The Bengal government repudiated it, and Goddard's march across India continued the war." } ],
  ['Salbai (Treaty 1782)',25.850,78.317,'Madhya Pradesh (Gwalior)','Ended First Anglo-Maratha War; Mahadji Scindia mediator; Salsette to British; 20 years\' peace.','treaty',
    { old: "Salbai (near Gwalior)", more: "Signed on 17 May 1782 (ratified 1783) between the EIC and the Marathas, with Mahadji Scindia as mediator. It ended the First Anglo-Maratha War: Salsette stayed with the British, Raghunathrao was pensioned, Madhavrao II was recognised as Peshwa, and territories west of the Yamuna were returned to Scindia. It secured about 20 years of peace between the British and Marathas." } ],
  ['Pollilur (1780, 1781)',12.967,79.700,'Tamil Nadu (Kanchipuram)','2nd Anglo-Mysore War: Hyder & Tipu destroyed Baillie\'s column 1780 — worst EIC defeat; Coote won 1781.','battle',
    { old: "Pullalur / Perambakam area (Kanchipuram); Pollilore", more: "On 10 September 1780, in the Second Anglo-Mysore War, Hyder Ali and Tipu Sultan destroyed Colonel William Baillie's column near Kanchipuram - one of the worst defeats of the EIC in India. Tipu's rockets played a role. A mural of the battle adorns Tipu's Daria Daulat Bagh at Srirangapatna. In August 1781 Sir Eyre Coote defeated Hyder on the same ground." } ],
  ['Porto Novo (1781)',11.492,79.766,'Tamil Nadu (Parangipettai, Cuddalore)','Eyre Coote defeated Hyder Ali in Second Anglo-Mysore War.','battle',
    { old: "Parangipettai (Tamil); Porto Novo (\"new port\", Portuguese); Mahmud Bandar", more: "On 1 July 1781 Sir Eyre Coote defeated Hyder Ali at Porto Novo on the Coromandel coast, the first major British success of the Second Anglo-Mysore War, relieving pressure on Madras. Porto Novo had been a Portuguese and later Dutch trading post. Hyder Ali died in December 1782 and Tipu continued the war." } ],
  ['Mangalore (Treaty 1784)',12.872,74.843,'Karnataka (Dakshina Kannada)','Ended Second Anglo-Mysore War between Tipu & EIC — mutual restitution of conquests.','treaty',
    { old: "Mangalore → Mangaluru (2014); Kodial (Konkani)", more: "Signed on 11 March 1784 between Tipu Sultan and the EIC, ending the Second Anglo-Mysore War on the basis of mutual restitution of conquests and release of prisoners. Tipu had besieged Mangalore for months. It was the last time an Indian power dictated terms on equal footing; Warren Hastings criticised the Madras government for it." } ],
  ['Srirangapatna (1792, 1799)',12.424,76.690,'Karnataka (Mandya)','Treaty of Seringapatam 1792 (Tipu lost half his kingdom); Tipu killed 4 May 1799 in Fourth Anglo-Mysore War.','battle',
    { old: "Seringapatam (colonial); Patan (Tipu's capital)", more: "Island capital of Hyder Ali and Tipu Sultan on the Kaveri. The Treaty of Seringapatam (March 1792), dictated by Cornwallis after the Third Anglo-Mysore War, took half of Tipu's territory and two sons as hostages. In the Fourth Anglo-Mysore War Tipu died defending the fort on 4 May 1799. The Wodeyars were restored under subsidiary alliance. Survives: Daria Daulat Bagh, Gumbaz, Jama Masjid." } ],
  ['Hyderabad (Subsidiary Alliance 1798)',17.362,78.475,'Telangana','Nizam first to sign Wellesley\'s Subsidiary Alliance (1798); French-trained troops disbanded.','treaty',
    { old: "Bhagnagar (traditional, attributed); Farkhunda Bunyad (Mughal title)", more: "Nizam Ali Khan (Asaf Jah II) became the first ruler to sign Lord Wellesley's Subsidiary Alliance on 1 September 1798, disbanding his French-trained corps under Raymond's successors and accepting a British force paid by the state. Further treaties (1800) ceded territories in lieu of payment (the \"Ceded Districts\"). Hyderabad remained the largest princely state until Operation Polo (1948)." } ],
  ['Bassein (Treaty 1802)',19.330,72.815,'Maharashtra (Vasai, Palghar)','Peshwa Baji Rao II accepted Subsidiary Alliance — triggered Second Anglo-Maratha War.','treaty',
    { old: "Bassein (Baçaim) → Vasai", more: "Signed on 31 December 1802 by Peshwa Baji Rao II, who had fled Pune after Yashwantrao Holkar's victory at Hadapsar. He accepted the Subsidiary Alliance and a British force, surrendering the Maratha confederacy's independence in foreign affairs. Scindia and Bhonsle regarded it as a betrayal, leading to the Second Anglo-Maratha War (1803-05)." } ],
  ['Assaye (1803)',20.235,75.890,'Maharashtra (Jalna)','Arthur Wellesley defeated Scindia & Bhonsle — Second Anglo-Maratha War.','battle',
    { old: "Assaye (village near Bhokardan, Jalna)", more: "On 23 September 1803 Major-General Arthur Wellesley (later Duke of Wellington) defeated the combined armies of Daulat Rao Scindia and Raghuji Bhonsle II. Wellesley later called it his finest victory. With Argaon/Adgaon (Nov 1803) it led to the Treaties of Deogaon (Bhonsle, ceding Cuttack) and Surji-Anjangaon (Scindia)." } ],
  ['Laswari (1803)',27.550,76.933,'Rajasthan (Alwar)','Gen. Lake crushed Scindia\'s army; Delhi & Agra came under British control.','battle',
    { old: "Laswari / Naswari (Alwar)", more: "On 1 November 1803 General Gerard Lake destroyed Scindia's European-trained northern army (the remnants of de Boigne's brigades). Following Lake's capture of Aligarh, Delhi (Battle of Delhi, Sept 1803) and Agra, this victory brought the Mughal emperor Shah Alam II under British protection and the Doab under EIC control." } ],
  ['Bharatpur — Lohagarh Fort',27.220,77.495,'Rajasthan','Jat fort repulsed Lake\'s four assaults 1805; finally stormed by Combermere 1826.','battle',
    { old: "Lohagarh (\"iron fort\"); Bharatpur (Jat capital of Suraj Mal)", more: "Built by Maharaja Suraj Mal in the 18th century, with massive mud ramparts and a moat. In 1805 Lord Lake made four unsuccessful assaults during the war with Holkar, who had taken refuge with Raja Ranjit Singh of Bharatpur; a treaty followed. In January 1826 Lord Combermere stormed the fort after mining its walls, ending its legend of invincibility." } ],
  ['Nalapani — Khalanga (1814)',30.393,78.085,'Uttarakhand (Dehradun)','Anglo-Nepal War: Gorkhas under Balbhadra Kunwar held the fort; Gen. Gillespie killed.','battle',
    { old: "Khalanga fort (Gorkha name)", more: "In October-November 1814, at the start of the Anglo-Nepal War, about 600 Gorkhas under Captain Balbhadra Kunwar held the hill fort of Khalanga near Dehradun against a British force of several thousand. Major-General Rollo Gillespie was killed in the first assault. The British erected memorials both to Gillespie and to \"our gallant adversary\" Balbhadra - a rare honour." } ],
  ['Sugauli (Treaty 1816)',26.762,84.741,'Bihar (East Champaran)','Ended Anglo-Nepal War; Nepal ceded Kumaon, Garhwal, Sikkim tracts; British resident at Kathmandu.','treaty',
    { old: "Segowlee (colonial)", more: "Signed on 2 December 1815 and ratified 4 March 1816, ending the Anglo-Nepal War (1814-16). Nepal ceded Kumaon, Garhwal, Sikkim tracts and much of the Terai, and accepted a British Resident in Kathmandu. The Mahakali (Kali) river became the western boundary - the basis of the present Kalapani-Lipulekh dispute. Gurkhas began to be recruited into the British Indian army." } ],
  ['Kirkee (Khadki) (1817)',18.564,73.851,'Maharashtra (Pune)','Third Anglo-Maratha War: Peshwa Baji Rao II\'s army defeated, 5 Nov 1817; Pune occupied.','battle',
    { old: "Kirkee (colonial) → Khadki", more: "On 5 November 1817, in the Third Anglo-Maratha War, Peshwa Baji Rao II attacked the British Residency near Pune; the British under Colonel Burr and Mountstuart Elphinstone defeated his army at Kirkee. Pune was occupied on 17 November. Baji Rao fled, surrendered in 1818, and was pensioned to Bithoor; the Peshwaship was abolished." } ],
  ['Koregaon (1818)',18.646,74.059,'Maharashtra (Pune)','1 Jan 1818: EIC force incl. Mahar sepoys held off Peshwa\'s army; Vijay Stambh memorial.','battle',
    { old: "Koregaon Bhima", more: "On 1 January 1818 about 800-900 EIC troops, largely Mahar soldiers of the Bombay Native Infantry under Captain Francis Staunton, held off a much larger Peshwa army for a day at Koregaon on the Bhima. The British erected the Vijay Stambh obelisk. Dr B.R. Ambedkar visited it in 1927, and it became a Dalit commemoration site; violence occurred at the bicentenary in 2018." } ],
  ['Mahidpur (1817)',23.487,75.657,'Madhya Pradesh (Ujjain)','Holkar army defeated 21 Dec 1817; Treaty of Mandsaur 1818 made Indore subsidiary.','battle',
    { old: "Mahidpur (Ujjain district)", more: "On 21 December 1817 Sir Thomas Hislop and Sir John Malcolm defeated the Holkar army of minor Malhar Rao Holkar III on the Shipra river. By the Treaty of Mandsaur (6 January 1818) Holkar ceded territories, gave up claims over Rajput states and accepted a subsidiary force, ending Holkar independence during the Third Anglo-Maratha War." } ],
  ['Nagpur — Sitabuldi',21.148,79.085,'Maharashtra','Bhonsle defeated at Sitabuldi Nov 1817; Nagpur annexed 1853–54 under Doctrine of Lapse.','annexation-capital',
    { old: "Sitabuldi hill (Nagpur); Nagpur (Bhonsle capital)", more: "On 26-27 November 1817 Appa Sahib Bhonsle of Nagpur attacked the British Residency on Sitabuldi hill and was defeated; he was beaten again at Nagpur in December. Part of the Third Anglo-Maratha War. After Raghuji III died without a natural heir, Dalhousie annexed Nagpur under the Doctrine of Lapse (1853-54); it became part of the Central Provinces in 1861." } ],
  ['Yandabo (Treaty 1826)',21.637,95.364,'Myanmar (Mandalay Region)','Ended First Anglo-Burmese War: Burma ceded Assam, Manipur, Arakan, Tenasserim.','treaty',
    { old: "Yandabo (village on the Irrawaddy)", more: "Signed on 24 February 1826, ending the First Anglo-Burmese War (1824-26) under Governor-General Lord Amherst. Burma (Ava) ceded Assam, Manipur, Arakan and Tenasserim, renounced claims on Cachar and Jaintia, paid an indemnity of £1 million and accepted a British Resident. It brought Assam under British rule, leading to the Ahom kingdom's end." } ],
  ['Madikeri (Coorg)',12.421,75.740,'Karnataka (Kodagu)','Coorg annexed 1834 by Bentinck, deposing Raja Chikka Virarajendra.','annexation-capital',
    { old: "Mercara (colonial); Muddurajakeri (after Haleri Raja Muddu Raja)", more: "Capital of the Haleri dynasty of Coorg (Kodagu), founded by Muddu Raja in 1681. In April 1834 Lord William Bentinck annexed Coorg after a short campaign against Raja Chikka Virarajendra, accused of misrule, who was exiled. The Madikeri fort and Omkareshwara temple survive. Coorg became a Chief Commissioner's province and later a Part C state (1950-56)." } ],
  ['Hyderabad (Sindh)',25.379,68.368,'Pakistan (Sindh)','Talpur Mirs\' capital; Napier won at Miani & Dubba nearby (Feb–Mar 1843); Sind annexed 1843 (Ellenborough).','annexation-capital',
    { old: "Neroon Kot (pre-Talpur town, attributed); Hyderabad (named by Kalhora ruler Ghulam Shah, 1768)", more: "Capital of the Talpur Mirs of Sindh. In 1843 Charles Napier defeated the Mirs at Miani (17 Feb) and Dubba near Hyderabad (24 Mar), and Lord Ellenborough annexed Sindh - an annexation widely condemned as unjust; Napier's supposed pun \"Peccavi\" is legend. Sindh was attached to the Bombay Presidency until 1936." } ],
  ['Mudki (1845)',30.783,74.883,'Punjab (Firozpur)','18 Dec 1845 — first battle of First Anglo-Sikh War; British victory (Gough).','battle',
    { old: "Moodkee (colonial)", more: "On 18 December 1845 Sir Hugh Gough and Governor-General Sir Henry Hardinge defeated a Sikh force under Lal Singh at Mudki in the first battle of the First Anglo-Sikh War. The Sikh army had crossed the Sutlej in December 1845. Treachery by the Sikh commanders Lal Singh and Tej Singh is widely blamed for the Khalsa defeats." } ],
  ['Ferozeshah (1845)',30.883,74.783,'Punjab (Firozpur)','21–22 Dec 1845, First Anglo-Sikh War; costly British victory.','battle',
    { old: "Ferozeshah / Pheru Shahr", more: "On 21-22 December 1845 Gough and Hardinge attacked the entrenched Sikh camp at Ferozeshah. The battle was extremely costly for the British and nearly lost; the Sikh second army under Tej Singh withdrew instead of pressing its advantage. It was one of the hardest-fought battles of British India." } ],
  ['Aliwal (1846)',30.945,75.614,'Punjab (Ludhiana)','28 Jan 1846: Harry Smith defeated Ranjodh Singh Majithia on the Sutlej.','battle',
    { old: "Aliwal (Ludhiana district)", more: "On 28 January 1846 Sir Harry Smith defeated the Sikh force under Ranjodh Singh Majithia near the Sutlej, removing the threat to Ludhiana. It was regarded as a model \"textbook\" battle. Combined with Sobraon, it ended the First Anglo-Sikh War." } ],
  ['Sobraon (1846)',31.183,74.850,'Punjab (Tarn Taran)','10 Feb 1846: decisive defeat ending First Anglo-Sikh War.','battle',
    { old: "Sobraon", more: "On 10 February 1846 Gough's army stormed the Sikh entrenchments at Sobraon on the Sutlej; many Sikh soldiers drowned when the bridge of boats broke. It was the decisive battle of the First Anglo-Sikh War. The Treaty of Lahore (March 1846) followed, ceding the Jalandhar Doab and imposing an indemnity." } ],
  ['Lahore (Treaties 1846; annexation 1849)',31.550,74.344,'Pakistan (Punjab)','Treaty of Lahore (Mar 1846) & Bhairowal (Dec 1846); Punjab annexed 29 Mar 1849 by Dalhousie.','treaty',
    { old: "Lahore; Lavapura (traditional)", more: "By the Treaty of Lahore (9 March 1846) the Sikhs ceded the Jalandhar Doab, paid an indemnity and reduced their army; Kashmir was separately sold to Gulab Singh by the Treaty of Amritsar. The Treaty of Bhairowal (December 1846) placed Punjab under a British Resident (Henry Lawrence) during minor Duleep Singh's rule. Dalhousie annexed Punjab on 29 March 1849." } ],
  ['Amritsar (Treaties 1809 & 1846)',31.640,74.860,'Punjab','1809 Metcalfe treaty fixed Sutlej as Ranjit Singh\'s border; 1846 Kashmir sold to Gulab Singh for Rs 75 lakh.','treaty',
    { old: "Ramdaspur / Chak Ramdas (early name, attributed)", more: "The Treaty of Amritsar (25 April 1809) between Charles Metcalfe and Maharaja Ranjit Singh fixed the Sutlej as the boundary, keeping the cis-Sutlej states under British protection. The second Treaty of Amritsar (16 March 1846) sold Kashmir and adjoining hill territories to Gulab Singh Dogra for Rs 75 lakh, creating the princely state of Jammu and Kashmir." } ],
  ['Multan (1848–49)',30.198,71.470,'Pakistan (Punjab)','Diwan Mulraj\'s revolt (Apr 1848) sparked Second Anglo-Sikh War; siege ended Jan 1849.','battle',
    { old: "Mulasthana / Mulasthanapura (ancient, attributed)", more: "In April 1848 Diwan Mulraj, governor of Multan, rebelled after two British officers, Vans Agnew and Anderson, were killed; the revolt spread and became the Second Anglo-Sikh War. Herbert Edwardes and later General Whish besieged Multan, which fell in January 1849 after a magazine explosion. Mulraj was tried and transported." } ],
  ['Chillianwala (1849)',32.650,73.600,'Pakistan (Punjab, Mandi Bahauddin)','13 Jan 1849: bloody, indecisive battle of Second Anglo-Sikh War.','battle',
    { old: "Chillianwala / Chillianwallah", more: "On 13 January 1849 Gough attacked the Sikh army of Sher Singh Attariwala near the Jhelum. Fighting was bloody and indecisive; the British suffered heavy losses, causing an outcry in Britain and Gough's planned replacement by Charles Napier. It was one of the costliest British battles in India." } ],
  ['Gujrat (1849)',32.574,74.079,'Pakistan (Punjab)','21 Feb 1849 "battle of the guns": final Sikh defeat; Punjab annexed.','battle',
    { old: "Gujrat (Punjab, Pakistan) - not to be confused with Gujarat state", more: "On 21 February 1849 Gough used massed artillery (\"the battle of the guns\") to defeat Sher Singh and Chattar Singh decisively. The Sikh army surrendered at Rawalpindi in March. Dalhousie annexed Punjab on 29 March 1849; Maharaja Duleep Singh was pensioned off and the Koh-i-Noor diamond was taken to Queen Victoria." } ],
  ['Satara',17.688,74.006,'Maharashtra','1848 — first state annexed under Dalhousie\'s Doctrine of Lapse.','annexation-capital',
    { old: "Satara (\"seven forts\"); Ajinkyatara fort", more: "Satara was the seat of Chhatrapati Shahu and his successors, the nominal heads of the Maratha state. After Raja Shahaji (Appa Sahib) died in 1848 without a natural heir, Dalhousie refused to recognise his adopted son and annexed Satara - the first application of the Doctrine of Lapse. Jaitpur and Sambalpur (1849), Udaipur (Chhattisgarh), Nagpur and Jhansi followed." } ],
  ['Sambalpur',21.470,83.970,'Odisha','Annexed 1849 under Doctrine of Lapse; Surendra Sai later led resistance.','annexation-capital',
    { old: "Sambalaka (Ptolemy, attributed); Hirakhand (diamond tract)", more: "Seat of a Chauhan kingdom; annexed by Dalhousie in 1849 under the Doctrine of Lapse after Raja Narayan Singh died without a male heir. Veer Surendra Sai, a claimant to the throne, led a long resistance against the British (1827-62) and joined the 1857 revolt; he died in Asirgarh jail in 1884. Hirakud dam lies nearby." } ],
  ['Jhansi',25.449,78.570,'Uttar Pradesh','Annexed 1854 under Doctrine of Lapse after Gangadhar Rao\'s death; adopted son refused.','annexation-capital',
    { old: "Balwantnagar (old name, attributed)", more: "After Raja Gangadhar Rao died in November 1853, Dalhousie refused to accept his adopted son Damodar Rao and annexed Jhansi in 1854 under the Doctrine of Lapse. Rani Lakshmibai's grievance led her to join the 1857 revolt; Sir Hugh Rose stormed Jhansi in April 1858. She was killed fighting near Gwalior (Kotah-ki-Serai) on 17/18 June 1858." } ],
  ['Thanjavur (Tanjore)',10.787,79.138,'Tamil Nadu','Subsidiary/cession 1799 (Serfoji II); Maratha kingdom extinguished 1855 for lack of male heir.','annexation-capital',
    { old: "Tanjore (colonial); Tanjavur", more: "Capital of the Cholas (Rajaraja I's Brihadisvara temple, UNESCO), later the Nayaks and the Thanjavur Marathas (from 1674, Venkoji/Ekoji). In 1799 Serfoji II ceded administration of the kingdom to the EIC, retaining the fort and a pension; he founded the Saraswathi Mahal Library. When Shivaji II died in 1855 without a male heir, Dalhousie's successor annexed the titles under the Doctrine of Lapse." } ],
  ['Lucknow (Awadh annexed 1856)',26.850,80.950,'Uttar Pradesh','Awadh annexed 7 Feb 1856 on "misgovernance" (Outram); Wajid Ali Shah exiled to Calcutta.','annexation-capital',
    { old: "Lakhanpur / Lakshmanpur (traditional, attributed)", more: "Nawab Asaf-ud-Daula moved the capital of Awadh from Faizabad to Lucknow in 1775. Dalhousie annexed Awadh on 7 February 1856 on grounds of misgovernance, based on James Outram's report, rather than lapse - the Nawab had heirs. Wajid Ali Shah was exiled to Metiabruz, Calcutta. Annexation angered sepoys (many from Awadh) and taluqdars, contributing to the 1857 revolt." } ]
 ]
});

GEO.add({
 id:'his-1857', group:'his-mod', title:'Revolt of 1857', bn:'১৮৫৭-র মহাবিদ্রোহ', icon:'🔥', world:false,
 intro:'কেন্দ্র → নেতা → দমনকারী ব্রিটিশ সেনাপতি — তিনটে একসঙ্গে মনে রাখো (দিল্লি–নিকলসন, কানপুর–ক্যাম্পবেল, ঝাঁসি–হিউ রোজ)।',
 types:{'centre-leader':['Centre & leader','#c62828'], outbreak:['Outbreak / first sparks','#ef6c00'], suppression:['Suppression / end','#455a64']},
 pts:[
  ['Barrackpore',22.760,88.370,'West Bengal (North 24 Parganas)','Mangal Pandey (34th BNI) attacked officers 29 Mar 1857, hanged 8 Apr; also 1824 sepoy mutiny.','outbreak',
    { old: "Chanak (local name); Barrackpore (\"barracks\", from 1772 cantonment)", more: "Barrackpore was the first British cantonment in India (1772) and the Governor-General's country retreat. In 1824 the 47th BNI mutinied here against overseas service to Burma and was suppressed with gunfire. On 29 March 1857 Mangal Pandey of the 34th BNI attacked his officers on the parade ground; he was hanged on 8 April and the regiment was disbanded. Mangal Pandey Udyan commemorates him." } ],
  ['Berhampore (Baharampur)',24.100,88.250,'West Bengal (Murshidabad)','19th BNI refused Enfield cartridges, Feb 1857 — first open refusal; regiment disbanded.','outbreak',
    { old: "Berhampore (colonial); Brahmapur", more: "A British cantonment near the Nawabi capital Murshidabad, built after Plassey. On 26 February 1857 the 19th Bengal Native Infantry refused to accept the new Enfield rifle cartridges, rumoured to be greased with cow and pig fat - the first open act of collective refusal. The regiment was marched to Barrackpore and disbanded on 31 March 1857." } ],
  ['Meerut',28.980,77.710,'Uttar Pradesh','10 May 1857: sepoys freed jailed comrades, killed officers, marched to Delhi — revolt began.','outbreak',
    { old: "Mayarashtra (traditional, attributed to Mayasura); Meerut (colonial spelling)", more: "On 9 May 1857, 85 sepoys of the 3rd Light Cavalry who refused the Enfield cartridges were publicly degraded and imprisoned. On 10 May (a Sunday) the sepoys broke into jail, freed them, killed British officers and civilians and marched overnight to Delhi. This began the Revolt of 1857. Meerut also has an Ashokan pillar (moved to Delhi by Firoz Shah Tughlaq)." } ],
  ['Delhi — Red Fort',28.656,77.241,'Delhi','Bahadur Shah Zafar proclaimed leader 11 May; Bakht Khan commanded; British recaptured Sept 1857 (Nicholson killed).','centre-leader',
    { old: "Qila-i-Mubarak (Mughal name); Shahjahanabad (walled city)", more: "The rebels reached Delhi on 11 May 1857 and proclaimed the aged Bahadur Shah II (Zafar) Emperor of Hindustan, the symbolic head of the revolt; Bakht Khan of Bareilly later commanded the rebel army. The British besieged Delhi from the Ridge and stormed it in September 1857; John Nicholson was killed. Hodson captured Zafar at Humayun's Tomb and shot his sons. Red Fort: UNESCO 2007." } ],
  ['Kanpur',26.4499,80.3319,'Uttar Pradesh','Nana Sahib & Tatya Tope; Wheeler\'s entrenchment, Satichaura Ghat & Bibighar killings; retaken by Havelock/Campbell.','centre-leader',
    { old: "Cawnpore (colonial) → Kanpur (1948)", more: "Under Nana Sahib and his commanders Tatya Tope and Azimullah Khan, rebels besieged General Hugh Wheeler's entrenchment in June 1857. The surrendering British were attacked at Satichaura Ghat (27 June) and the women and children killed at the Bibighar (15 July). Havelock retook the city on 17 July; Brigadier Neill's brutal reprisals followed. Campbell defeated Tatya Tope here in December 1857." } ],
  ['Bithoor',26.613,80.272,'Uttar Pradesh (Kanpur)','Seat of Nana Sahib, adopted son of Peshwa Baji Rao II whose pension was stopped.','centre-leader',
    { old: "Brahmavarta (traditional); Bithur", more: "A Ganga-side pilgrimage town where the last Peshwa, Baji Rao II, lived in exile after 1818 on a British pension. His adopted son Nana Sahib (Dhondu Pant) was refused the pension by Dalhousie after Baji Rao's death (1851) and led the revolt at Kanpur in 1857. Rani Lakshmibai (Manikarnika) grew up here. Havelock's troops destroyed Nana Sahib's palace." } ],
  ['Lucknow — Residency',26.862,80.926,'Uttar Pradesh','Begum Hazrat Mahal (son Birjis Qadr); Residency siege, Henry Lawrence killed; relieved by Campbell, Mar 1858.','centre-leader',
    { old: "British Residency, Lucknow (built 1780-1800 for the Resident at the Awadh court)", more: "From 30 June 1857 rebels besieged the British in the Residency; Chief Commissioner Sir Henry Lawrence was killed on 4 July. Havelock and Outram reinforced it in September; Sir Colin Campbell evacuated it in November. Begum Hazrat Mahal led the Awadh revolt in the name of her son Birjis Qadr. Campbell recaptured Lucknow in March 1858. The ruins are preserved as a memorial." } ],
  ['Jhansi Fort',25.458,78.575,'Uttar Pradesh','Rani Lakshmibai led the revolt; Hugh Rose stormed Jhansi April 1858.','centre-leader',
    { old: "Balwantnagar (old name of Jhansi, attributed); Bangra hill fort", more: "Built by Raja Bir Singh Deo of Orchha in 1613 on Bangra hill. After the Doctrine of Lapse annexation (1854), Rani Lakshmibai took control in 1857. Sir Hugh Rose besieged and stormed Jhansi in April 1858, defeating Tatya Tope's relief army at the Betwa; the Rani escaped on horseback with her adopted son to Kalpi." } ],
  ['Kalpi',26.120,79.730,'Uttar Pradesh (Jalaun)','Rani Lakshmibai & Tatya Tope regrouped; defeated by Hugh Rose, May 1858.','centre-leader',
    { old: "Kalapriya / Kalpi (traditional); Kalpi fort on the Yamuna", more: "An old fort town on the Yamuna, a base of the Peshwa's Bundelkhand officers. After the fall of Jhansi, Rani Lakshmibai, Tatya Tope and Rao Sahib regrouped at Kalpi, which had an arsenal. Sir Hugh Rose defeated them at Kunch and took Kalpi on 23 May 1858. The rebels then marched to seize Gwalior. Kalpi is traditionally the birthplace of Ved Vyasa." } ],
  ['Gwalior',26.213,78.178,'Madhya Pradesh','Rebels seized Gwalior; Rani Lakshmibai killed fighting 17–18 June 1858 (Kotah-ki-Serai); Hugh Rose retook it.','suppression',
    { old: "Gopadri / Gopachala (ancient hill name); Gwalior fort (\"Gibraltar of India\")", more: "On 1 June 1858 the rebels under Tatya Tope, Rao Sahib and Rani Lakshmibai won over Scindia's army and seized Gwalior; Maharaja Jayajirao Scindia fled to Agra. Rani Lakshmibai was killed fighting the 8th Hussars at Kotah-ki-Serai on 17/18 June 1858; Hugh Rose recaptured Gwalior on 19-20 June. Her samadhi is at Phool Bagh, Gwalior." } ],
  ['Shivpuri',25.430,77.650,'Madhya Pradesh','Tatya Tope, betrayed by Man Singh, hanged here 18 April 1859.','suppression',
    { old: "Sipri (colonial); summer capital of the Scindias", more: "After a long guerrilla campaign across central India, Tatya Tope (Ramachandra Pandurang) was betrayed by Man Singh, Raja of Narwar, captured in the Paron forest in April 1859, tried by a military court and hanged at Shivpuri on 18 April 1859. Shivpuri later became the Scindias' summer capital; Madhav National Park lies nearby." } ],
  ['Bareilly',28.367,79.431,'Uttar Pradesh','Khan Bahadur Khan ruled Rohilkhand in Mughal name; captured & hanged 1860.','centre-leader',
    { old: "Bans Bareli (traditional); capital of Rohilkhand (Rohilla Afghans)", more: "Khan Bahadur Khan, a grandson of the Rohilla chief Hafiz Rahmat Khan, assumed control of Rohilkhand on 31 May 1857 as viceroy of Bahadur Shah Zafar and organised an administration. Bakht Khan marched from Bareilly to Delhi with rebel troops. Campbell recaptured Bareilly in May 1858; Khan Bahadur Khan fled to Nepal, was handed over and hanged in 1860." } ],
  ['Faizabad',26.772,82.146,'Uttar Pradesh (Ayodhya)','Maulvi Ahmadullah Shah led Awadh rebels; killed at Powayan, June 1858.','centre-leader',
    { old: "Bangla (early name); first capital of the Nawabs of Awadh (till 1775)", more: "Faizabad was the first capital of the Nawabs of Awadh. In 1857 Maulvi Ahmadullah Shah (\"Danka Shah\", the \"Lighthouse of the Rebellion\") led rebels in Awadh, fighting at Lucknow and Shahjahanpur. He was killed by the Raja of Powayan in June 1858 for a British reward. The district was renamed Ayodhya in 2018." } ],
  ['Jagdishpur',25.467,84.417,'Bihar (Bhojpur)','Kunwar Singh, 80-year-old zamindar, led Bihar revolt; died April 1858 after victory near Jagdishpur.','centre-leader',
    { old: "Jagdishpur estate (Ujjainiya Rajputs)", more: "Babu Kunwar Singh, the nearly 80-year-old Rajput zamindar of Jagdishpur, took command of the Danapur mutineers in July 1857. He fought across Bihar, Awadh and central India in a long guerrilla campaign. Wounded while crossing the Ganga (he is said to have cut off his injured arm), he defeated Captain Le Grand near Jagdishpur on 23 April 1858 and died on 26 April. 23 April is observed as Vijayotsav in Bihar." } ],
  ['Arrah House',25.557,84.665,'Bihar (Bhojpur)','Danapur sepoys & Kunwar Singh besieged Arrah House July 1857; relieved by Vincent Eyre.','outbreak',
    { old: "Arrah (Ara), Shahabad district (colonial)", more: "In late July 1857 the Danapur sepoys with Kunwar Singh besieged about 68 Britons and Sikh police in a small two-storey building at Arrah belonging to railway engineer Richard Vicars Boyle. A relief column under Captain Dunbar was ambushed and routed. Major Vincent Eyre lifted the siege on 3 August 1857 after eight days." } ],
  ['Allahabad — Khusro Bagh',25.442,81.821,'Uttar Pradesh (Prayagraj)','Maulvi Liaqat Ali proclaimed rule from Khusro Bagh, June 1857; Neill\'s brutal reprisals.','centre-leader',
    { old: "Prayag; Ilahabad/Allahabad (Akbar, 1575) → Prayagraj (2018)", more: "Khusro Bagh is a Mughal walled garden with the tombs of Prince Khusrau (Jahangir's son), his mother Shah Begum and sister Sultan Nithar Begum. In June 1857 Maulvi Liaqat Ali, a weaver-preacher, proclaimed himself governor for Bahadur Shah Zafar and ran the rebel administration from here. Colonel James Neill retook Allahabad with brutal mass hangings. Liaqat Ali was captured in 1871." } ],
  ['Patna',25.594,85.138,'Bihar','Pir Ali, bookbinder, led rising; hanged July 1857 by Commissioner William Tayler.','centre-leader',
    { old: "Pataliputra (Mauryan), Kusumapura, Azimabad (18th c.)", more: "In 1857 Pir Ali, a Lucknow-born bookbinder in Patna, organised a rising and on 3 July 1857 his followers killed Dr Lyall, an opium agent. Commissioner William Tayler arrested and hanged him with others on 7 July 1857. Tayler's harsh methods led to his removal. Patna was earlier the Mauryan capital Pataliputra." } ],
  ['Ranchi',23.360,85.330,'Jharkhand','Thakur Vishwanath Shahdeo & Pandey Ganpat Rai led Chota Nagpur revolt; both hanged 1858.','centre-leader',
    { old: "Archi / Ranchi (Oraon village name, attributed)", more: "In 1857 Thakur Vishwanath Shahdeo of Barkagarh and his commander Pandey Ganpat Rai led the revolt in Chota Nagpur after the Ramgarh battalion mutinied at Hazaribagh and Doranda (Ranchi). They held Ranchi for some weeks. Both were captured and hanged in April 1858. Shaheed Sheikh Bhikhari and Tikait Umrao Singh were other martyrs of the region." } ],
  ['Sambalpur',21.470,83.970,'Odisha','Surendra Sai waged guerrilla war 1857–62; surrendered 1862, died in Asirgarh jail.','centre-leader',
    { old: "Sambalaka (Ptolemy, attributed); Hirakhand", more: "Veer Surendra Sai, a Chauhan claimant to the Sambalpur throne, had been jailed at Hazaribagh since 1840. Freed by mutineers in July 1857, he waged guerrilla war with his brothers in the hills of western Odisha for years. He surrendered in 1862, was later re-arrested (1864) and died in Asirgarh fort jail in 1884." } ],
  ['Kota',25.180,75.830,'Rajasthan','Kota troops under Jaidayal & Haridayal killed Political Agent Major Burton, Oct 1857.','centre-leader',
    { old: "Kotah (colonial); Kota (Hada Chauhan state)", more: "In October 1857 the troops of the Kota state under Lala Jaidayal and Mehrab Khan (Haridayal also named) rose and killed the Political Agent Major Charles Burton and his sons; they held the Maharao virtually captive for six months. British forces under General Roberts recaptured Kota in March 1858. Jaidayal and Mehrab Khan were later hanged." } ],
  ['Indore',22.719,75.855,'Madhya Pradesh','Saadat Khan led Holkar\'s troops against the British Residency, 1 July 1857.','centre-leader',
    { old: "Indrapur / Indreshwar (traditional, after Indreshwar temple); Holkar capital", more: "On 1 July 1857 Holkar's troops under Saadat Khan attacked the British Residency at Indore; Resident Henry Durand withdrew to Sehore. Maharaja Tukoji Rao Holkar II stayed loyal to the British. Saadat Khan was captured years later and hanged at the Residency in 1874." } ],
  ['Banda',25.483,80.333,'Uttar Pradesh','Nawab Ali Bahadur II led Bundelkhand rebels; joined Rani & Tatya Tope.','centre-leader',
    { old: "Banda (Bundelkhand); Bamdeo (traditional, after sage Vamdeva)", more: "Nawab Ali Bahadur II of Banda, a descendant of Peshwa Bajirao I and Mastani, joined the revolt in June 1857 and led Bundelkhand rebels, cooperating with Rani Lakshmibai and Tatya Tope. General Whitlock defeated him at Banda in April 1858; he surrendered and was pensioned to Indore." } ],
  ['Jorhat',26.750,94.220,'Assam','Maniram Dewan (tea pioneer) & Piyali Barua plotted Ahom restoration; hanged Feb 1858.','centre-leader',
    { old: "Jorhat (\"two markets\"); last capital of the Ahom kingdom (late 18th c.)", more: "Maniram Dutta Baruah (Maniram Dewan), an early Assamese tea planter and former ally of the British, conspired in 1857 to restore the Ahom prince Kandarpeswar Singha with the help of sepoys. The plot was discovered; Maniram and Piyali Baruah were hanged at Jorhat jail on 26 February 1858. He is remembered as the first Assamese tea planter and a martyr." } ],
  ['Rewari',28.180,76.620,'Haryana','Rao Tula Ram led Ahirwal rebels; defeated at Nasibpur (Narnaul), Nov 1857.','centre-leader',
    { old: "Rewari (Ahirwal); Rewa-wadi (traditional, attributed)", more: "Rao Tula Ram of Rampura, the Ahir chief of Rewari, seized the area in May 1857, raised troops and sent help to Delhi. He fought the British at Nasibpur near Narnaul on 16 November 1857, where he was defeated. He later went to Afghanistan to seek foreign help and died in Kabul in 1863. Haryana marks 23 September as Rao Tula Ram's martyrdom day." } ],
  ['Rangoon (Yangon)',16.797,96.145,'Myanmar','Bahadur Shah Zafar exiled after trial 1858; died 1862, buried here — end of Mughal dynasty.','suppression',
    { old: "Dagon (ancient); Rangoon (British, from Yan Kon) → Yangon (1989)", more: "After his trial in the Red Fort (January-March 1858) for rebellion, Bahadur Shah Zafar was exiled to Rangoon with his wife Zinat Mahal. He died on 7 November 1862 and was buried in an unmarked grave; the grave was rediscovered in 1991 and is now a dargah. His death ended the Mughal dynasty. Rangoon was capital of British Burma from 1852 and of Myanmar till 2005." } ]
 ]
});

GEO.add({
 id:'his-revolts', group:'his-mod', title:'Tribal, Peasant & Early Revolts', bn:'আদিবাসী, কৃষক ও প্রাথমিক বিদ্রোহ', icon:'🏹', world:false,
 intro:'বিদ্রোহের নাম + নেতা + সাল + অঞ্চল — চতুষ্টয় মুখস্থ করো; ছোটনাগপুর আর উত্তর-পূর্বে আদিবাসী বিদ্রোহের ঘনত্ব ম্যাপে দেখো।',
 types:{tribal:['Tribal uprising','#2e7d32'], peasant:['Peasant movement','#8d6e63'], 'civil-religious':['Civil / religious / chieftain revolt','#ef6c00'], military:['Military mutiny','#455a64']},
 pts:[
  ['Mahasthangarh (Sanyasi–Fakir revolt)',24.961,89.343,'Bangladesh (Bogra)','Sanyasi–Fakir revolt 1763–1800 in north Bengal; Majnu Shah\'s refuge & battles 1771, 1785; inspired Anandamath.','civil-religious',
    { old: "Pundranagara / Pundravardhana (Mauryan-Gupta city)", more: "Mahasthangarh, the ancient city of Pundranagara (a Mauryan Brahmi inscription found here), lay in the zone of the Sanyasi-Fakir rebellion (c. 1763-1800), when wandering Hindu sanyasis and Muslim fakirs, angered by EIC restrictions on pilgrimage and heavy revenue after the 1770 Bengal famine, raided Company posts. Leaders included Majnu Shah Burhana, Musa Shah, Bhawani Pathak and Debi Chaudhurani. Bankim Chandra's Anandamath (1882), with \"Vande Mataram\", is set in this revolt." } ],
  ['Raipur (Chuar uprising)',22.800,86.950,'West Bengal (Bankura)','Chuar rising 1798–99 of Jungle Mahal under Durjan Singh, zamindar of Raipur.','tribal',
    { old: "Jungle Mahals (colonial district, 1805); Chuar = \"uncivilised\" (colonial term for Bhumij/tribals)", more: "The Chuar risings (1766-1816) were revolts of Bhumij and other tribal peasants and dispossessed zamindars of the Jungle Mahals (Midnapore, Bankura, Manbhum) against EIC revenue demands. The major phase of 1798-99 was led by Durjan Singh, zamindar of Raipur, whose estate had been auctioned. The British created the Jungle Mahals district in 1805 partly in response. Rani Shiromani of Karnagarh is another noted leader." } ],
  ['Khurda (Paika Rebellion)',20.180,85.620,'Odisha (Khordha)','Paika Rebellion 1817 under Bakshi Jagabandhu against EIC land revenue policy.','civil-religious',
    { old: "Khurda / Khordha (last independent kingdom of Odisha, Bhoi dynasty)", more: "The Paikas were the hereditary militia of the Raja of Khurda, whose lands were taken after the British conquest of Odisha (1803). In 1817 they rose under Bakshi Jagabandhu Bidyadhar against loss of rent-free lands, high revenue and salt price rises, attacking Company offices; the revolt spread to Puri. It was suppressed by 1818-19; Jagabandhu surrendered in 1825. The Odisha government calls it the \"first war of independence\"." } ],
  ['Kittur',15.599,74.788,'Karnataka (Belagavi)','Rani Chennamma resisted Doctrine-of-Lapse-like annexation 1824; Sangolli Rayanna fought on.','civil-religious',
    { old: "Kittur (Kitturu) - seat of the Desai chiefs", more: "After Raja Shivalingarudra Sarja died in 1824, the EIC refused to recognise his adopted son - an anticipation of the Doctrine of Lapse. Rani Chennamma resisted; in October 1824 her forces killed the Dharwad Collector St John Thackeray. The British captured Kittur in December 1824 and she died in Bailhongal prison in 1829. Sangolli Rayanna continued guerrilla resistance until his hanging in 1831." } ],
  ['Kundara (Velu Thampi)',8.957,76.673,'Kerala (Kollam)','Kundara Proclamation, Jan 1809 — Travancore Dalawa Velu Thampi called people to revolt against EIC.','civil-religious',
    { old: "Kundara (Quilon district, colonial)", more: "Velu Thampi Dalawa, Dewan of Travancore, turned against the EIC over heavy subsidy demands and Resident Colonel Macaulay's interference. On 11 January 1809 he issued the Kundara Proclamation at the Elampalloor temple, calling on people to rise against the British. After defeats, he took his own life at Mannadi in 1809. Paliath Achan of Cochin joined him." } ],
  ['Kayathar (Polygar revolt)',8.950,77.800,'Tamil Nadu (Thoothukudi)','Veerapandiya Kattabomman of Panchalankurichi hanged here 16 Oct 1799 — First Polygar War.','civil-religious',
    { old: "Panchalankurichi (Kattabomman's fort, nearby)", more: "Veerapandiya Kattabomman, the Palayakkarar (polygar) of Panchalankurichi, refused to pay tribute to the EIC. After a clash with Collector Jackson (1798) and Major Bannerman's siege of Panchalankurichi (1799), he fled, was betrayed by the Raja of Pudukottai, and was hanged at Kayathar on 16 October 1799. The Second Polygar War (1800-01) followed under the Maruthu brothers and Oomaithurai." } ],
  ['Vellore Fort (1806 mutiny)',12.921,79.128,'Tamil Nadu','10 July 1806 sepoy mutiny against new turban & caste-mark ban; Tipu\'s sons interned here.','military',
    { old: "Vellore (Velur); fort of the Vijayanagara Aravidu chiefs (16th c.)", more: "On 10 July 1806 Indian sepoys of the Madras Army rose at Vellore, killing about 100 British officers and soldiers, protesting against a new dress code by Commander-in-Chief John Cradock that banned caste marks and earrings and introduced a round hat resembling Christian headgear. Tipu Sultan's family, interned in the fort, was suspected of instigating it. Colonel Gillespie crushed it from Arcot. Governor Lord William Bentinck was recalled." } ],
  ['Ranchi (Kol uprising)',23.360,85.330,'Jharkhand','Kol uprising 1831–32 across Chota Nagpur (Ranchi, Singhbhum) under Buddhu Bhagat.','tribal',
    { old: "Kol (colonial collective term for Ho, Munda, Oraon); Chota Nagpur", more: "The Kol uprising (1831-32) arose from the transfer of tribal lands to outsiders (dikus) - Sikh, Muslim and Hindu traders and moneylenders - with British backing. Led by Buddhu Bhagat, Joa Bhagat and others, it spread across Ranchi, Singhbhum, Hazaribagh, Palamau and Manbhum. After suppression the British created the South-West Frontier Agency (1833) with a special administration." } ],
  ['Barabazar (Bhumij revolt)',23.032,86.366,'West Bengal (Purulia)','Bhumij revolt 1832–33 led by Ganga Narayan Singh of Barabhum (Ganga Narayan Hangama).','tribal',
    { old: "Barabhum (zamindari, Manbhum district)", more: "Ganga Narayan Singh of the Barabhum zamindari family led the Bhumij revolt of 1832-33 (the \"Ganga Narayan Hangama\") against British revenue policy, the Company-backed zamindar and abuses by police and moneylenders. The rebels killed the dewan Madhab Singh and attacked police stations. He was killed in 1833 while fighting the Thakur of Kharsawan. The revolt led to administrative changes in the Jungle Mahals." } ],
  ['Bhanjanagar (Ghumsur — Khond uprising)',19.925,84.582,'Odisha (Ganjam)','Khond uprising 1837–56 under Chakra Bisoi against suppression of meriah (human sacrifice).','tribal',
    { old: "Russellkonda (British, after George Russell) → Bhanjanagar; Ghumsur (Gumsur) estate", more: "The Khonds (Kandhas) of the Ghumsur hill tracts rose between 1837 and 1856, led first by Dora Bisoi and then Chakra Bisoi, against British interference, new taxes and the campaign to suppress the meriah (human sacrifice) ritual by the Meriah Agency (Campbell, Macpherson). Russellkonda town was named after Commissioner George Russell. Chakra Bisoi disappeared, never captured." } ],
  ['Bhognadih (Santhal Hul)',24.874,87.593,'Jharkhand (Sahibganj)','Santhal Hul June 1855–56 led by Sidhu & Kanhu Murmu; Santhal Parganas district created 1855.','tribal',
    { old: "Damin-i-Koh (British-demarcated Santhal tract, 1832)", more: "On 30 June 1855 about 10,000 Santhals gathered at Bhognadih where brothers Sidhu and Kanhu Murmu (with Chand, Bhairav, Phulo and Jhano) declared the Hul against zamindars, moneylenders (mahajans) and Company officials in the Damin-i-Koh. The rising was crushed by 1856 with heavy loss of life. The British created the Santhal Parganas district (Non-Regulation) in December 1855. 30 June is observed as Hul Divas." } ],
  ['Ulihatu (Munda Ulgulan)',22.983,85.491,'Jharkhand (Khunti)','Birsa Munda\'s birthplace; Ulgulan 1899–1900 (Dombari Buru); led to Chota Nagpur Tenancy Act 1908.','tribal',
    { old: "Ulihatu (Munda village, Ranchi district in colonial times)", more: "Birsa Munda was born at Ulihatu on 15 November 1875 (date traditionally given). He founded the Birsait faith and led the Ulgulan (\"great tumult\", 1899-1900) against the breakdown of the khuntkatti (collective landholding) system, begari (forced labour), dikus and missionaries. The rebels were massacred at Dombari Buru (January 1900). Birsa died in Ranchi jail in June 1900. The Chota Nagpur Tenancy Act 1908 followed; 15 November is Janjatiya Gaurav Divas." } ],
  ['Jorhat (Ahom revolt)',26.750,94.220,'Assam','Ahom prince Gomdhar Konwar rose against the British in 1828 after Yandabo.','civil-religious',
    { old: "Jorhat (\"two markets\"); last Ahom capital (late 18th c.)", more: "After the Treaty of Yandabo (1826) the British stayed on in Assam instead of withdrawing. In 1828 Gomdhar Konwar, an Ahom prince, with Dhanjay Borgohain, declared himself king near Jorhat and tried to expel the British; the rising was quickly suppressed. A second plot in 1830 also failed. The British partly restored Upper Assam to Purandar Singha (1833-38) before annexing it." } ],
  ['Krishnanagar (Indigo revolt)',23.400,88.500,'West Bengal (Nadia)','Indigo revolt 1859–60 in Nadia (Digambar & Bishnu Biswas); Dinabandhu Mitra\'s Neel Darpan; Indigo Commission 1860.','peasant',
    { old: "Krishnanagar (after Raja Krishnachandra Roy); Nadia / Navadvipa region", more: "In 1859-60 indigo cultivators of Nadia, Jessore, Pabna and other Bengal districts refused to grow indigo under the coercive tinkathia-like system of European planters. Digambar and Bishnu Biswas of Chaugachha led the first refusals. Harish Chandra Mukherjee's Hindoo Patriot supported them; Dinabandhu Mitra's play Neel Darpan (1860) exposed planters and was translated by Rev. James Long. The Indigo Commission (1860) followed." } ],
  ['Pabna',24.008,89.239,'Bangladesh','Pabna Agrarian League 1873 against zamindars; led to Bengal Tenancy Act 1885.','peasant',
    { old: "Pabna; Yusufshahi pargana (scene of the 1873 rising)", more: "In 1873 peasants of the Yusufshahi pargana of Pabna formed an Agrarian League against zamindars who were enhancing rents illegally and evicting tenants. The movement used legal means - rent strikes and court cases - and was largely non-violent and non-communal, supported by R.C. Dutt, Surendranath Banerjee and the Indian Association. It contributed to the Bengal Tenancy Act of 1885." } ],
  ['Malerkotla (Kuka movement)',30.517,75.883,'Punjab','Kuka (Namdhari) rebels blown from cannons Jan 1872; Ram Singh Kuka exiled to Rangoon.','civil-religious',
    { old: "Maler + Kotla (Afghan principality, 15th c.)", more: "The Kuka (Namdhari) movement founded by Baba Ram Singh at Bhaini Sahib (1857) preached Sikh purification and swadeshi, boycotting British goods and services. In January 1872 a band of Kukas attacked Malerkotla over cow-slaughter issues; Deputy Commissioner L. Cowan had 49 (and later 16 more) blown from cannons without trial. Ram Singh was exiled to Rangoon, where he died in 1885." } ],
  ['Faridpur (Faraizi movement)',23.602,89.833,'Bangladesh','Faraizi movement of Haji Shariatullah & Dudu Miyan — peasants vs zamindars & indigo planters, 19th c.','civil-religious',
    { old: "Fatehabad (Mughal name, attributed); Faridpur after saint Shah Farid", more: "Haji Shariatullah (1781-1840), born in Faridpur, founded the Faraizi movement (from c. 1818) calling Bengali Muslims to observe obligatory duties (faraiz) and reject innovations. His son Dudu Miyan (Muhsinuddin) turned it into an agrarian movement, organising peasants against zamindars' illegal cesses and indigo planters and setting up a parallel administration. It declined after Dudu Miyan's death in 1862." } ],
  ['Patna (Wahabi centre)',25.594,85.138,'Bihar','HQ of Wahabi movement (Sayyid Ahmad Barelvi\'s followers); crushed in Patna conspiracy trials 1864–65.','civil-religious',
    { old: "Pataliputra (Mauryan), Kusumapura, Azimabad (18th c.)", more: "Patna was the main Indian centre of the Wahabi (Tariqah-i-Muhammadiya) movement founded by Sayyid Ahmad Barelvi (killed at Balakot, 1831), with Wilayat Ali and Inayat Ali as leaders. Patna supplied men and money to the frontier base at Sittana. In the Ambala (1864) and Patna (1865) conspiracy trials, leaders were transported; the movement was crushed by the 1870s." } ],
  ['Rampachodavaram (Rampa rebellions)',17.439,81.777,'Andhra Pradesh (East Godavari)','Rampa revolt 1879 of Koyas; Alluri Sitarama Raju\'s guerrilla Rampa rebellion 1922–24.','tribal',
    { old: "Rampa (hill tract / estate), Godavari Agency", more: "The Rampa revolt of 1879-80 by Koya and Konda Dora hill tribes was against the mansabdar's exactions, police oppression and new forest and toddy regulations. In 1922-24 Alluri Sitarama Raju led a guerrilla rebellion in the same Rampa-Gudem hills, attacking police stations (Chintapalli, Krishnadevipeta). He was captured and shot on 7 May 1924 at Koyyuru. His rebellion coincided with the Non-Cooperation movement era." } ],
  ['Bijolia',25.164,75.325,'Rajasthan (Bhilwara)','Bijolia kisan movement 1897–1941 against cesses; Vijay Singh Pathik led from 1916.','peasant',
    { old: "Bijolia / Bijoliya (thikana of Mewar); Uparmal plateau", more: "Peasants (mainly Dhakar) of the Bijolia thikana in Mewar protested from 1897 against dozens of cesses (lag-bag). Led by Sadhu Sitaram Das and from 1916 by Vijay Singh Pathik, later Manikya Lal Verma and Haribhau Upadhyay, it became one of the longest peasant movements, lasting till c. 1941. Bijolia also has 12th-century Chahamana inscriptions and temples." } ],
  ['Jagdalpur (Bhumkal)',19.080,82.020,'Chhattisgarh (Bastar)','Bhumkal rebellion 1910 of Bastar tribes led by Gunda Dhur.','tribal',
    { old: "Jagdalpur - capital of the Kakatiya-Bhanj rulers of Bastar state", more: "The Bhumkal rebellion of 1910 in Bastar state was a tribal uprising against forest reservation, restriction of shifting cultivation, forced labour, and the dewan and police oppression. Led by Gunda Dhur of Nethanar, the rebels attacked markets, police stations and officials. It was suppressed by British troops; Gunda Dhur was never captured and is a folk hero of Bastar." } ],
  ['Mangarh (Bhil)',23.350,73.987,'Rajasthan (Banswara)','17 Nov 1913 massacre of Bhils of Govind Guru\'s Bhagat movement — "Adivasi Jallianwala".','tribal',
    { old: "Mangarh / Mangadh hill (Gujarat-Rajasthan border)", more: "Govind Guru (Govindgiri) led a reformist Bhagat movement among the Bhils of the Banswara-Santrampur region, advocating temperance and resisting forced labour. On 17 November 1913 British-led troops fired on thousands of Bhils gathered on Mangarh hill, killing hundreds (estimates vary widely, up to 1,500). It is called the \"Adivasi Jallianwala\"; Govind Guru was arrested and exiled." } ],
  ['Gumla (Tana Bhagat)',23.044,84.542,'Jharkhand','Tana Bhagat movement 1914 of Oraons under Jatra Bhagat; later joined Gandhian struggle.','tribal',
    { old: "Gumla (Ranchi district in colonial times)", more: "Started in 1914 by Jatra Bhagat (Jatra Oraon) among the Oraons of Chota Nagpur, the Tana Bhagat movement called for monotheism, abstinence from meat and liquor, and refusal to pay rent and do forced labour. In the 1920s Tana Bhagats joined the Congress-led non-cooperation and satyagrahas, adopting Gandhian ideals; they still wear the khadi cap and hoist the tricolour." } ],
  ['Pratapgarh (Awadh Kisan Sabha)',25.897,81.945,'Uttar Pradesh','Baba Ramchandra organised Awadh kisans 1920; Awadh Kisan Sabha formed Oct 1920.','peasant',
    { old: "Belha (old name of Pratapgarh town); Pratapgarh (after Raja Pratap Singh, 17th c.)", more: "Baba Ramchandra, a former indentured labourer in Fiji, organised tenants of Pratapgarh, Rae Bareli and Sultanpur against bedakhli (eviction), nazrana and begar from 1920. Jawaharlal Nehru visited the villages in June 1920. The Awadh Kisan Sabha was set up at Pratapgarh in October 1920 by Ramchandra, Gauri Shankar Misra, Nehru and others; it was later broken up by repression." } ],
  ['Hardoi (Eka movement)',27.420,80.120,'Uttar Pradesh','Eka (unity) movement 1921–22 of tenants under Madari Pasi.','peasant',
    { old: "Hardoi (Hardwi, traditional association with Hiranyakashipu)", more: "At the end of 1921 the Eka (\"unity\") movement emerged in Hardoi, Bahraich, Barabanki and Sitapur districts of Awadh, protesting high rents (often 50% above recorded), oppressive cesses and the practice of share-rents. Started by Congress, it was led by Madari Pasi and other low-caste leaders and was not committed to non-violence. Repression crushed it by March 1922." } ],
  ['Tirurangadi (Moplah rebellion)',11.050,75.930,'Kerala (Malappuram)','Malabar (Moplah) rebellion Aug 1921; Wagon tragedy Nov 1921.','peasant',
    { old: "Ernad-Valluvanad taluks (Malabar district, Madras Presidency)", more: "The Malabar (Moplah/Mappila) rebellion began in August 1921 after police tried to arrest Khilafat leaders at Tirurangadi, where the Mamburam shrine stands. Led by Variyankunnath Kunjahammed Haji, Ali Musliyar and others, it combined agrarian anger against landlords with the Khilafat movement but turned communal in places. In the \"Wagon tragedy\" (20 November 1921), 64 prisoners suffocated in a closed railway goods wagon." } ],
  ['Tamenglong (Zeliangrong)',24.990,93.500,'Manipur','Heraka movement: Jadonang (hanged 1931) & Rani Gaidinliu (arrested 1932; "Rani" title by Nehru).','tribal',
    { old: "Tamenglong (Naga hills of Manipur); Kambiron (Jadonang's village nearby)", more: "Haipou Jadonang of Kambiron started the Heraka religious-political movement among the Zeliangrong Nagas, aiming at a \"Naga Raj\" free of British rule. He was hanged in Imphal on 29 August 1931. His 16-year-old cousin Gaidinliu continued the struggle, was arrested in 1932 and jailed till 1947. Nehru called her \"Rani\" (queen of the Nagas). She received the Padma Bhushan (1982)." } ],
  ['Talasari (Warli revolt)',20.123,72.916,'Maharashtra (Palghar)','Warli adivasi revolt 1945 under Godavari Parulekar (Kisan Sabha) against forced labour.','tribal',
    { old: "Thana district (colonial; now Palghar)", more: "In 1945 Warli adivasis of Thana district, organised by Godavari Parulekar and Shamrao Parulekar of the Kisan Sabha (CPI), rose against forced labour (veth-begar), debt bondage and exploitation by landlords and forest contractors. The movement won better wages and an end to bonded labour practices. Godavari Parulekar's book \"Jevha Manus Jaga Hoto\" describes the struggle." } ],
  ['Dinajpur (Tebhaga)',25.629,88.637,'Bangladesh','Tebhaga movement 1946–47 — sharecroppers demanded 2/3 share; strongest in Dinajpur (incl. Thakurgaon).','peasant',
    { old: "Dinajpur (Bengal Presidency)", more: "In 1946-47 sharecroppers (bargadars) of Bengal, organised by the Bengal Provincial Kisan Sabha (CPI), demanded that their share of the crop be raised from one-half to two-thirds (tebhaga) and that the crop be stored in their own barns. It was strongest in Dinajpur, Rangpur, Jalpaiguri, Midnapore and 24 Parganas. The Bargadars Bill (1947) was introduced; the movement faded after Partition." } ],
  ['Punnapra–Vayalar',9.433,76.333,'Kerala (Alappuzha)','Oct 1946 communist-led uprising against Travancore Diwan C.P. Ramaswami Iyer.','peasant',
    { old: "Ambalappuzha-Cherthala region (Travancore state)", more: "In October 1946 communist-led coir workers and agricultural labourers in Punnapra and Vayalar (Alappuzha) rose against the Travancore Dewan Sir C.P. Ramaswami Iyer and his \"American model\" constitution plan for an independent Travancore. The uprising was brutally suppressed by the state army with hundreds killed. It is commemorated as a landmark in Kerala's left politics." } ],
  ['Nalgonda (Telangana struggle)',17.058,79.268,'Telangana','Telangana armed peasant struggle 1946–51 against Nizam\'s jagirdars & Razakars.','peasant',
    { old: "Nalgonda (Nilagiri, traditional) in Hyderabad State", more: "The Telangana armed struggle (1946-51) was led by the Communists through the Andhra Mahasabha against the feudal deshmukhs, jagirdars, vetti (forced labour) and the Nizam's Razakars. It began after the killing of Doddi Komarayya (July 1946) at Kadavendi; Chakali Ailamma was a noted figure. Peasants controlled about 3,000 villages. After Operation Polo (1948) the Indian Army suppressed it; it was withdrawn in 1951." } ]
 ]
});

GEO.add({
 id:'his-reform', group:'his-mod', title:'Socio-Religious Reform & Education Centres', bn:'সমাজ-ধর্ম সংস্কার ও শিক্ষাকেন্দ্র', icon:'📚', world:false,
 intro:'সংস্থা + প্রতিষ্ঠাতা + সাল + শহর — বাংলা, মহারাষ্ট্র, পাঞ্জাব, কেরলের সংস্কার আন্দোলন পাশাপাশি তুলনা করো।',
 types:{'reform-movement':['Reform movement / society','#6a1b9a'], 'education-press':['College / university / press','#00838f']},
 pts:[
  ['Kolkata (College Street)',22.576,88.362,'West Bengal','Asiatic Society 1784; Fort William College 1800; Hindu College 1817 (Derozio\'s Young Bengal); Brahmo Samaj 1828.','reform-movement',
    { old: "Kalikata → Calcutta → Kolkata (2001)", more: "Calcutta was the cradle of the Bengal Renaissance. William Jones founded the Asiatic Society in 1784; Wellesley set up Fort William College in 1800 to train civil servants in Indian languages. Hindu College (1817, now Presidency University) on College Street produced Derozio's radical Young Bengal. Raja Rammohan Roy founded the Brahmo Sabha (1828, later Brahmo Samaj); Calcutta University came in 1857." } ],
  ['Serampore',22.752,88.351,'West Bengal (Hooghly)','Baptist Mission Press 1800 (Carey); Samachar Darpan 1818; Serampore College 1818.','education-press',
    { old: "Frederiksnagore (Danish, 1755-1845); Srirampur", more: "Under Danish protection, William Carey, Joshua Marshman and William Ward set up the Baptist Mission Press in 1800, printing the Bible in over 40 Indian languages and Bengali prose works. They published Digdarshan and the Bengali weekly Samachar Darpan (1818) and the English Friend of India, and founded Serampore College (1818), which received a Danish royal charter to grant degrees (1827)." } ],
  ['Belur Math',22.633,88.356,'West Bengal (Howrah)','Ramakrishna Mission HQ (Vivekananda, mission 1897, math 1898); Dakshineswar temple across river.','reform-movement',
    { old: "Belur (Howrah); Dakshineswar (Rani Rashmoni's Kali temple, 1855) opposite", more: "Swami Vivekananda founded the Ramakrishna Mission on 1 May 1897 and established Belur Math on the Hooghly in 1898 as headquarters of the Ramakrishna Order, combining spiritual practice with social service (\"service to man is service to God\"). The main temple (1938) blends Hindu, Islamic and Christian architectural motifs. Ramakrishna Paramahamsa served at Dakshineswar across the river." } ],
  ['Shantiniketan',23.681,87.680,'West Bengal (Birbhum)','Tagore\'s Brahmacharya ashram 1901; Visva-Bharati 1921; UNESCO site 2023.','education-press',
    { old: "Bhubandanga (original village); Santiniketan ashram of Debendranath Tagore (1863)", more: "Debendranath Tagore set up an ashram here in 1863. Rabindranath Tagore started the Brahmacharya Ashram school in 1901, which grew into Visva-Bharati (founded 1921, central university 1951), with open-air classes, and Sriniketan for rural reconstruction. Nandalal Bose and Ramkinkar Baij developed the Santiniketan art tradition. Santiniketan became a UNESCO World Heritage Site in September 2023." } ],
  ['Mumbai',18.950,72.830,'Maharashtra','Rahnumai Mazdayasnan Sabha 1851; Prarthana Samaj 1867 (Atmaram Pandurang); Arya Samaj founded 10 Apr 1875.','reform-movement',
    { old: "Bom Baia (Portuguese) → Bombay → Mumbai (1995)", more: "The Rahnumai Mazdayasnan Sabha (1851) was founded by Naoroji Furdonji, Dadabhai Naoroji and S.S. Bengalee for Parsi reform. The Prarthana Samaj was founded in 1867 by Atmaram Pandurang with M.G. Ranade and R.G. Bhandarkar. Swami Dayanand Saraswati founded the Arya Samaj in Bombay on 10 April 1875. Bombay University (1857) and the first INC session (1885) were also here." } ],
  ['Pune',18.524,73.839,'Maharashtra','Phule\'s girls\' school 1848 & Satyashodhak Samaj 1873; Deccan Education Society 1884; Servants of India Society 1905.','reform-movement',
    { old: "Punyanagari / Punaka (Rashtrakuta-era name, attributed); Poona (colonial) → Pune (1978)", more: "Jyotiba and Savitribai Phule opened a girls' school at Bhide Wada in 1848 and founded the Satyashodhak Samaj in 1873 against caste hierarchy. Tilak, Agarkar and Chiplunkar founded the Deccan Education Society (1884) and Fergusson College (1885). G.K. Gokhale founded the Servants of India Society in 1905. Ranade's Poona Sarvajanik Sabha (1870) and D.K. Karve's women's university (1916) were also here." } ],
  ['Lahore',31.550,74.344,'Pakistan (Punjab)','DAV College 1886 (Hansraj, Arya Samaj); Lahore Singh Sabha 1879.','education-press',
    { old: "Lavapura / Lahaur (traditional, after Lava, son of Rama)", more: "After Dayanand's death (1883), Arya Samajists led by Lala Hansraj and Lala Lajpat Rai founded the Dayanand Anglo-Vedic (DAV) school and college in Lahore (1886) - the \"college party\" - while the Gurukul wing (Shraddhanand) split away. The Lahore Singh Sabha (1879) was a more radical Sikh reform body than Amritsar's. The 1929 INC session declaring Purna Swaraj was held here." } ],
  ['Amritsar — Khalsa College',31.634,74.836,'Punjab','Singh Sabha 1873; Khalsa College 1892; SGPC 1920 & Akali gurdwara reform movement.','reform-movement',
    { old: "Ramdaspur / Chak Guru (early names of Amritsar)", more: "The Sri Guru Singh Sabha was founded in Amritsar in 1873 to reform Sikh practice and promote education. Khalsa College (1892) became the leading Sikh educational institution. The Akali movement (1920-25) for control of gurdwaras from corrupt mahants led to the formation of the SGPC (November 1920) and the Sikh Gurdwaras Act of 1925. Nankana Sahib massacre (1921) and Guru-ka-Bagh morcha (1922) were part of it." } ],
  ['Aligarh',27.915,78.079,'Uttar Pradesh','Sir Syed Ahmad Khan\'s MAO College 1875, became AMU 1920 — centre of the Aligarh movement.','education-press',
    { old: "Koil / Kol (old name of Aligarh); Ramgarh, Sabitgarh (attributed)", more: "Sir Syed Ahmad Khan founded the Muhammadan Anglo-Oriental (MAO) school in 1875 (college from 1877), modelled on Oxford and Cambridge, to promote modern Western education among Muslims. His Scientific Society (1864) and journal Tahzib-ul-Akhlaq supported the Aligarh movement. MAO College became Aligarh Muslim University in 1920. Sir Syed advised Muslims to keep away from the INC." } ],
  ['Deoband',29.698,77.676,'Uttar Pradesh (Saharanpur)','Darul Uloom 1866 (Qasim Nanautavi, Rashid Gangohi) — anti-British orthodox revivalism.','reform-movement',
    { old: "Deoband (Devavana, traditional, attributed)", more: "Founded on 30 May 1866 by Muhammad Qasim Nanautavi and Rashid Ahmad Gangohi, Darul Uloom Deoband was a revivalist seminary teaching orthodox Islamic sciences and opposing British rule and Western education. Unlike Aligarh, it welcomed the INC; Mahmud-ul-Hasan led the Silk Letter conspiracy (1913-20). The Jamiat Ulama-i-Hind (1919) grew from Deobandi ulama." } ],
  ['Lucknow — Nadwatul Ulama',26.865,80.933,'Uttar Pradesh','Nadwatul Ulama 1894 (Muhammad Ali Mungeri, Shibli Nomani) — blend of tradition & modern learning.','education-press',
    { old: "Nadwat-ul-Ulama (\"council of scholars\"), founded at Kanpur", more: "The Nadwatul Ulama was founded in 1893-94 at Kanpur by Maulana Muhammad Ali Mungeri, with Shibli Nomani among its leading figures, and its seminary moved to Lucknow (1898). It sought a middle path between Deoband's orthodoxy and Aligarh's westernisation, combining classical Islamic learning with modern subjects and Arabic as a living language. Abul Hasan Ali Nadwi later headed it." } ],
  ['Qadian',31.819,75.376,'Punjab (Gurdaspur)','Ahmadiyya movement founded 1889 by Mirza Ghulam Ahmad.','reform-movement',
    { old: "Islampur Qazi (Mughal-era name, attributed)", more: "Mirza Ghulam Ahmad (1835-1908) of Qadian founded the Ahmadiyya movement in 1889, claiming to be the Promised Messiah and Mahdi. It promoted liberal Islamic ideas and Western education and opposed jihad by violence. Orthodox Muslims regard it as heretical; Pakistan declared Ahmadis non-Muslims in 1974. Qadian remains the movement's spiritual centre in India." } ],
  ['Haridwar — Gurukul Kangri',29.922,78.128,'Uttarakhand','Gurukul founded 1902 by Swami Shraddhanand (Arya Samaj, Gurukul wing).','education-press',
    { old: "Kangri village (original Gurukul site, Bijnor side of the Ganga)", more: "Swami Shraddhanand (Munshi Ram), leader of the Gurukul wing of the Arya Samaj, founded the Gurukul at Kangri village near Haridwar in 1902 to impart Vedic education in the ancient guru-shishya tradition, with Hindi as medium. It later shifted to its present campus at Haridwar and became a deemed university (1962). Shraddhanand also led the shuddhi movement and was assassinated in 1926." } ],
  ['Delhi — Jamia Millia Islamia',28.563,77.283,'Delhi','Founded at Aligarh 1920 (Non-Cooperation/Khilafat); moved to Delhi 1925.','education-press',
    { old: "Jamia Millia Islamia (\"national Muslim university\"), founded at Aligarh", more: "Founded at Aligarh on 29 October 1920 by Maulana Mahmud-ul-Hasan, Muhammad Ali Jauhar, Hakim Ajmal Khan, Dr M.A. Ansari and others in response to Gandhi's call to boycott government-aided institutions during Non-Cooperation and Khilafat. It moved to Karol Bagh, Delhi in 1925 and later to Okhla. Dr Zakir Husain headed it for many years. It became a central university in 1988." } ],
  ['Varanasi — BHU',25.268,82.989,'Uttar Pradesh','Besant\'s Central Hindu College 1898 grew into Banaras Hindu University 1916 (Madan Mohan Malaviya).','education-press',
    { old: "Kashi; Banaras / Benares → Varanasi (1956)", more: "Annie Besant founded the Central Hindu College at Varanasi in 1898. Pandit Madan Mohan Malaviya, with the support of Besant and the Maharaja of Darbhanga, established Banaras Hindu University by the BHU Act 1915; its foundation stone was laid by Lord Hardinge on 4 February 1916, when Gandhi gave a famous speech. The Central Hindu College became its nucleus." } ],
  ['Ahmedabad — Gujarat Vidyapith',23.035,72.568,'Gujarat','National university founded by Gandhi 1920 during Non-Cooperation.','education-press',
    { old: "Karnavati / Ashaval (pre-1411 names); Ahmedabad (Ahmad Shah, 1411)", more: "Mahatma Gandhi founded the Gujarat Vidyapith on 18 October 1920 as a national university during the Non-Cooperation movement, to provide education free of government control, with Gujarati as medium, emphasis on khadi, crafts and village service. Gandhi was its lifelong chancellor. It became a deemed university in 1963." } ],
  ['Adyar — Theosophical Society',13.010,80.270,'Tamil Nadu (Chennai)','HQ from 1882 (Blavatsky & Olcott; society founded New York 1875); Besant\'s Home Rule League 1916.','reform-movement',
    { old: "Adyar (Madras); Huddleston Gardens estate", more: "The Theosophical Society was founded in New York in 1875 by Madame H.P. Blavatsky and Colonel H.S. Olcott; its international headquarters moved to Adyar, Madras, in 1882. It promoted the revival of Hinduism, Buddhism and Zoroastrianism and comparative religion. Annie Besant became president in 1907 and launched the Home Rule League in Madras in September 1916. Its banyan tree and library are famous." } ],
  ['Erode',11.341,77.717,'Tamil Nadu','Home town of E.V. Ramasamy "Periyar" — Self-Respect Movement 1925.','reform-movement',
    { old: "Kongu region; Erode (traditional, \"eru odai\" - two streams, attributed)", more: "E.V. Ramasamy \"Periyar\" was born at Erode in 1879. Leaving the Congress over caste discrimination at the Cheranmadevi gurukulam, he launched the Self-Respect Movement in 1925 against Brahminical dominance, caste and gender inequality, and founded the Dravidar Kazhagam in 1944. His Erode house is now a memorial museum. Erode is also called the \"Turmeric City\"." } ],
  ['Swamithope',8.120,77.490,'Tamil Nadu (Kanyakumari)','Ayya Vaikundar\'s Samathuva Samajam (c. 1830s) against caste oppression in Travancore.','reform-movement',
    { old: "Poovandanthoppe (earlier name, attributed); Swamithope (\"Swami's grove\")", more: "Swamithope is the headquarters of the Ayyavazhi faith founded by Ayya Vaikundar (Mudisoodum Perumal, c. 1809-51) in southern Travancore. He organised the Samathuva Samajam (c. 1836) to promote equality and community dining, opposed caste oppression of Nadars and others and the Travancore king's taxes, and was imprisoned by the king. The main shrine is Swamithope Pathi." } ],
  ['Aruvippuram',8.422,77.097,'Kerala (Thiruvananthapuram)','Narayana Guru consecrated a Shiva idol 1888 — challenged Brahmin monopoly; SNDP Yogam 1903.','reform-movement',
    { old: "Aruvippuram (on the Neyyar river)", more: "In 1888 Sree Narayana Guru consecrated a Shiva idol (a stone from the Neyyar) at Aruvippuram, challenging the Brahmin monopoly over temple consecration; he reportedly said he installed \"our Shiva\" (an Ezhava Shiva). The Aruvippuram temple association led to the formation of the Sree Narayana Dharma Paripalana (SNDP) Yogam in 1903 with Dr Palpu and Kumaran Asan. His motto: \"One caste, one religion, one God for man\"." } ],
  ['Venganoor',8.396,77.003,'Kerala (Thiruvananthapuram)','Ayyankali\'s home; villuvandi (bullock-cart) protest 1893; Sadhu Jana Paripalana Sangham 1907.','reform-movement',
    { old: "Venganoor (Travancore state)", more: "Ayyankali (1863-1941) was born at Venganoor in a Pulaya family. In 1893 he rode a villuvandi (decorated bullock cart) along public roads barred to Dalits, defying caste restrictions. He founded the Sadhu Jana Paripalana Sangham (1907), led an agricultural labourers' strike for school admission of Dalit children (1907-08) and the Kallumala (stone necklace) agitation. He became a member of the Sree Moolam Praja Sabha (1911)." } ],
  ['Varkala — Sivagiri',8.739,76.732,'Kerala (Thiruvananthapuram)','Narayana Guru\'s Sivagiri Mutt; his samadhi 1928; Sivagiri pilgrimage.','reform-movement',
    { old: "Varkala (Janardana temple town, \"Dakshina Kashi\")", more: "Sree Narayana Guru established the Sivagiri Mutt at Varkala in 1904 and the Sree Narayana Dharma Sangham (1928) there. He attained samadhi at Sivagiri on 20 September 1928. The annual Sivagiri pilgrimage (from 1932) is held from 30 December to 1 January, focusing on education, cleanliness, devotion, organisation, agriculture, trade and technical training. Tagore (1922) and Gandhi (1925) visited him." } ],
  ['Changanassery (NSS)',9.467,76.550,'Kerala (Kottayam)','Nair Service Society 1914 by Mannathu Padmanabhan, HQ Perunna.','reform-movement',
    { old: "Changanacherry (colonial spelling); Perunna (NSS HQ)", more: "Mannathu Padmanabhan founded the Nair Service Society on 31 October 1914 at Changanassery (headquarters at Perunna) to reform the Nair community: ending the matrilineal joint-family (marumakkathayam) disputes, extravagant ceremonies and polygamy, and promoting education. NSS later participated in the Vaikom and Guruvayur satyagrahas (Mannathu led the Savarna Jatha of 1924) and the 1959 \"Vimochana Samaram\"." } ],
  ['Vaikom',9.750,76.396,'Kerala (Kottayam)','Vaikom Satyagraha 1924–25 for temple-road entry; T.K. Madhavan; Periyar "Vaikom Veerar"; Gandhi visited.','reform-movement',
    { old: "Vaikom (Travancore state); Vaikom Mahadeva temple", more: "Started on 30 March 1924 to secure the right of avarnas (lower castes) to use public roads around the Vaikom Mahadeva temple, led by T.K. Madhavan, K.P. Kesava Menon and George Joseph with Congress support. Mannathu Padmanabhan led a Savarna Jatha; Periyar was jailed and called \"Vaikom Veerar\"; Gandhi visited in March 1925. In November 1925 the roads on three sides were opened." } ],
  ['Guruvayur',10.595,76.039,'Kerala (Thrissur)','Temple-entry satyagraha 1931–32 led by K. Kelappan, A.K. Gopalan as volunteer captain.','reform-movement',
    { old: "Guruvayur (Zamorin of Calicut's temple, Malabar district)", more: "From 1 November 1931 to October 1932, the Guruvayur Satyagraha sought temple entry for all Hindus at the Sri Krishna temple, then managed by the Zamorin. K. Kelappan (\"Kerala Gandhi\") led it; A.K. Gopalan was volunteer captain, and P. Krishna Pillai rang the temple bell. Kelappan's fast was ended at Gandhi's request. Full temple entry came only in 1947 after the Madras Temple Entry Authorization Act." } ]
 ]
});

GEO.add({
 id:'his-freedom', group:'his-mod', title:'National Movement 1885–1947', bn:'জাতীয় আন্দোলন ১৮৮৫–১৯৪৭', icon:'🇮🇳', world:false,
 intro:'স্বদেশি (১৯০৫) → গান্ধীর আঞ্চলিক সত্যাগ্রহ (১৯১৭–১৮) → অসহযোগ → আইন অমান্য → ভারত ছাড়ো → INA — ধাপে ধাপে ম্যাপে ঘুরে দেখো।',
 types:{gandhian:['Gandhian movement','#ef6c00'], 'revolutionary-ina':['Revolutionary & INA','#6a1b9a'], 'partition-swadeshi':['Partition & Swadeshi','#2e7d32'], 'other-event':['Other event / conference','#1565c0']},
 pts:[
  ['Kolkata — Town Hall',22.568,88.345,'West Bengal','Boycott resolution 7 Aug 1905 against Curzon\'s partition (16 Oct 1905); Alipore bomb case 1908; Writers\' Building attack 1930.','partition-swadeshi',
    { old: "Calcutta → Kolkata (2001)", more: "At a mass meeting in the Calcutta Town Hall on 7 August 1905 the formal resolution to boycott British goods was passed, launching the Swadeshi movement against Lord Curzon's Partition of Bengal, which took effect on 16 October 1905 (observed as a day of mourning; Tagore's Raksha Bandhan). Later events: the Alipore Bomb Case (1908, Aurobindo acquitted) and the Writers' Building attack by Benoy, Badal and Dinesh (8 December 1930)." } ],
  ['Barisal',22.700,90.367,'Bangladesh','Swadeshi stronghold (Aswini Kumar Dutta\'s Swadesh Bandhab Samiti); 1906 provincial conference lathi-charged.','partition-swadeshi',
    { old: "Barisal → Barishal (2018, official spelling); Chandradwip (medieval kingdom)", more: "Barisal was a stronghold of the Swadeshi movement under Aswini Kumar Dutta, whose Swadesh Bandhab Samiti organised boycott and arbitration courts across the district. In April 1906 the Bengal Provincial Conference at Barisal was broken up by police lathi-charge, with Surendranath Banerjee arrested. Mukunda Das's patriotic jatras also came from Barisal." } ],
  ['Thoothukudi',8.806,78.145,'Tamil Nadu','V.O. Chidambaram Pillai\'s Swadeshi Steam Navigation Co. 1906; Coral Mill strike 1908.','partition-swadeshi',
    { old: "Tuticorin (colonial)", more: "In 1906 V.O. Chidambaram Pillai founded the Swadeshi Steam Navigation Company to challenge the British India Steam Navigation Company's monopoly on the Tuticorin-Colombo route, buying ships Gallia and Lawoe. In 1908 he supported the Coral Mills strike; his arrest with Subramania Siva led to riots in Tirunelveli. He was given two life sentences (later reduced) and is called \"Kappalottiya Tamizhan\"." } ],
  ['Maniyachi',8.863,77.895,'Tamil Nadu (Thoothukudi)','Vanchinathan shot Tirunelveli Collector Ashe at the railway station, 17 June 1911.','revolutionary-ina',
    { old: "Maniyachi Junction (now Vanchi Maniyachi)", more: "On 17 June 1911 Vanchinathan (Vanchi Iyer), a member of the Bharata Matha Association, shot dead Robert Ashe, the Collector of Tirunelveli, at Maniyachi railway station, and then shot himself. Ashe had been involved in suppressing the Swadeshi movement of V.O. Chidambaram Pillai. The station was renamed Vanchi Maniyachi in his honour." } ],
  ['Muzaffarpur',26.123,85.391,'Bihar','Khudiram Bose & Prafulla Chaki bombed carriage meant for Kingsford, Apr 1908.','revolutionary-ina',
    { old: "Tirhut (district name); Muzaffarpur (after Muzaffar Khan, 18th c.)", more: "On 30 April 1908 Khudiram Bose and Prafulla Chaki of the Jugantar group threw a bomb at a carriage they believed carried District Judge Douglas Kingsford, known for harsh sentences in Calcutta; two British women were killed instead. Prafulla shot himself when cornered; Khudiram, aged 18, was hanged at Muzaffarpur on 11 August 1908. Tilak's articles defending them led to his six-year sentence." } ],
  ['Nashik',20.007,73.796,'Maharashtra','Abhinav Bharat: Anant Kanhere shot Collector Jackson 1909; Ambedkar\'s Kalaram temple-entry satyagraha 1930.','revolutionary-ina',
    { old: "Nasikya (Satavahana-era, Pandavleni caves); Gulshanabad (Mughal)", more: "Nashik was the base of V.D. Savarkar's Mitra Mela (1899), which became Abhinav Bharat (1904). On 21 December 1909 Anant Kanhere shot Collector A.M.T. Jackson at a theatre in Nashik; in the Nashik Conspiracy Case Kanhere, Karve and Deshpande were hanged and Savarkar was transported to the Andamans. In March 1930 Ambedkar led the Kalaram temple-entry satyagraha here." } ],
  ['Budge Budge',22.474,88.174,'West Bengal (South 24 Parganas)','Komagata Maru passengers fired upon on return, 29 Sept 1914 (Ghadar era).','revolutionary-ina',
    { old: "Baj Baj (local name of Budge Budge)", more: "The Komagata Maru, chartered by Gurdit Singh, carried 376 mainly Punjabi passengers to Vancouver in 1914 to challenge Canada's \"continuous journey\" rule; most were refused entry. On return, on 29 September 1914, police tried to force the passengers onto a train to Punjab at Budge Budge; firing killed about 20. The episode fuelled the Ghadar movement." } ],
  ['Motihari (Champaran)',26.650,84.917,'Bihar (East Champaran)','Champaran Satyagraha 1917 against tinkathia indigo system — Gandhi\'s first in India (Raj Kumar Shukla).','gandhian',
    { old: "Champaran (Champaranya, traditional); Motihari (district HQ)", more: "Raj Kumar Shukla persuaded Gandhi to visit Champaran, where European planters forced tenants to grow indigo on 3/20 of their land (tinkathia). Gandhi arrived at Motihari in April 1917 and defied an order to leave the district. With Rajendra Prasad, J.B. Kripalani, Mazharul Haq and others, he documented grievances. The Champaran Agrarian Act (1918) abolished tinkathia. George Orwell was born in Motihari (1903)." } ],
  ['Kheda',22.750,72.680,'Gujarat','Kheda Satyagraha 1918 for revenue remission after crop failure; Vallabhbhai Patel joined.','gandhian',
    { old: "Kaira (colonial) → Kheda", more: "In 1918 crops failed in Kheda district, but the government refused remission of land revenue under the Revenue Code rules. Gandhi launched a satyagraha urging peasants not to pay; Vallabhbhai Patel left his legal practice to join, with Indulal Yagnik. The government finally agreed that revenue be recovered only from those who could pay. It was Gandhi's first non-cooperation campaign in Gujarat." } ],
  ['Ahmedabad — Sabarmati Ashram',23.060,72.581,'Gujarat','Ashram 1917; mill strike 1918 (Gandhi\'s first hunger strike); Dandi March began 12 Mar 1930.','gandhian',
    { old: "Satyagraha Ashram / Harijan Ashram; Karnavati / Ashaval (old Ahmedabad)", more: "Gandhi founded the Satyagraha Ashram at Kochrab in 1915 and moved it to the Sabarmati riverbank in 1917. In 1918 he led the Ahmedabad mill workers' strike against the mill owners (Ambalal Sarabhai; his sister Anasuya Sarabhai supported workers), undertaking his first hunger strike in India; a 35% wage rise was awarded. On 12 March 1930 he set out on the Dandi March from here with 78 followers." } ],
  ['Amritsar — Jallianwala Bagh',31.621,74.881,'Punjab','Massacre 13 Apr 1919 by Gen. Dyer (Rowlatt protests, Kitchlew & Satyapal); Hunter Committee; Tagore returned knighthood.','gandhian',
    { old: "Jallianwala Bagh (garden of the Jallewalla family)", more: "On 13 April 1919 (Baisakhi), Brigadier-General Reginald Dyer ordered troops to fire without warning on a peaceful crowd protesting the arrest of Dr Saifuddin Kitchlew and Dr Satyapal under the Rowlatt Act; official figure 379 killed, others estimated far more. Tagore renounced his knighthood; the Hunter Committee inquired. Udham Singh shot Michael O'Dwyer in London in 1940. National memorial; renovated 2021." } ],
  ['Chauri Chaura',26.651,83.581,'Uttar Pradesh (Gorakhpur)','Police station burnt 5 Feb 1922 (22 policemen killed); Gandhi withdrew Non-Cooperation.','gandhian',
    { old: "Chauri and Chaura (twin villages)", more: "On 4/5 February 1922 police fired on Non-Cooperation volunteers protesting against liquor sales and high prices at Chauri Chaura; the crowd set the police station on fire, killing 22 (or 23) policemen. Gandhi called off the Non-Cooperation movement (Bardoli resolution, 12 February 1922), disappointing many leaders. Of those tried, 19 were eventually hanged after appeal (Madan Mohan Malaviya defended)." } ],
  ['Bardoli',21.120,73.120,'Gujarat (Surat)','Bardoli Satyagraha 1928 against revenue hike; women gave Vallabhbhai the title "Sardar".','gandhian',
    { old: "Bardoli taluka (Surat district)", more: "In 1928 the Bombay government raised land revenue in Bardoli taluka by about 22%. Vallabhbhai Patel organised a no-revenue campaign; the peasants (largely Patidars, also Kaliparaj tribals) resisted confiscations and social boycott. The Maxwell-Broomfield inquiry found the hike unjustified and reduced it to about 6%. The women of Bardoli gave Patel the title \"Sardar\". Bardoli was earlier chosen for mass civil disobedience in 1922." } ],
  ['Dandi',20.886,72.801,'Gujarat (Navsari)','Gandhi broke the salt law 6 Apr 1930 after 24-day march — Civil Disobedience began.','gandhian',
    { old: "Dandi (coastal village, Navsari, then Surat district)", more: "Gandhi marched about 385 km (240 miles) from Sabarmati Ashram with 78 volunteers (12 March - 5 April 1930) and on 6 April 1930 broke the salt law by picking up natural salt at Dandi, launching the Civil Disobedience Movement. The National Salt Satyagraha Memorial was inaugurated at Dandi in 2019. The salt tax issue had been raised in Gandhi's eleven demands to Lord Irwin." } ],
  ['Dharasana',20.683,72.917,'Gujarat (Valsad)','Salt works raid May 1930 (Sarojini Naidu, Imam Saheb); brutality reported by Webb Miller.','gandhian',
    { old: "Dharasana salt works (Bulsar district, colonial)", more: "After Gandhi's arrest on 5 May 1930, the planned raid on the Dharasana salt works was led by Abbas Tyabji (arrested), then Sarojini Naidu and Imam Saheb. On 21 May 1930 about 2,500 non-violent satyagrahis were beaten with lathis by police as they advanced. American journalist Webb Miller's eyewitness report spread worldwide sympathy for the Indian cause." } ],
  ['Vedaranyam',10.377,79.850,'Tamil Nadu (Nagapattinam)','C. Rajagopalachari\'s salt march from Tiruchirappalli, April 1930.','gandhian',
    { old: "Thirumaraikkadu (Tamil, \"forest of the Vedas\"); Vedaranyam", more: "In April 1930 C. Rajagopalachari (Rajaji) led about 100 satyagrahis on a salt march from Tiruchirappalli to Vedaranyam on the Tanjore coast, where he broke the salt law on 30 April 1930 and was arrested. It was the Tamil counterpart of the Dandi March. Sardar Vedaratnam Pillai assisted locally. Vedaranyam is also a Shaivite shrine town." } ],
  ['Payyanur',12.108,75.194,'Kerala (Kannur)','K. Kelappan led Malabar salt satyagraha from Calicut to Payyanur, 1930.','gandhian',
    { old: "Payyannur (Chirakkal taluk, Malabar district)", more: "In April 1930 K. Kelappan led a batch of satyagrahis on foot from Calicut to Payyanur, where they broke the salt law on 23 April 1930 - the Kerala salt satyagraha. T.R. Krishnaswami Iyer also led a group from Palakkad. Payyanur had hosted the 1928 Kerala Provincial Congress conference, chaired by Nehru, which first demanded complete independence in Kerala." } ],
  ['Peshawar — Qissa Khwani Bazaar',34.008,71.570,'Pakistan (Khyber Pakhtunkhwa)','Firing on Khudai Khidmatgars (Ghaffar Khan) 23 Apr 1930; Garhwal Rifles (Chandra Singh Garhwali) refused to fire.','gandhian',
    { old: "Purushapura (ancient Peshawar, Kushan capital); Qissa Khwani (\"storytellers' market\")", more: "On 23 April 1930, after Khan Abdul Ghaffar Khan's arrest, British troops fired on unarmed Khudai Khidmatgars (\"Red Shirts\") and others in Qissa Khwani Bazaar, Peshawar, killing many (estimates vary). Two platoons of the Royal Garhwal Rifles under Havildar Chandra Singh Garhwali refused to fire on the crowd and were court-martialled. Peshawar was out of British control for some days." } ],
  ['Pune — Yerawada Jail',18.565,73.890,'Maharashtra','Poona Pact 1932 (Gandhi–Ambedkar) signed after fast here; Gandhi interned at Aga Khan Palace 1942–44.','gandhian',
    { old: "Yeravda / Yerwada; Poona (colonial) → Pune", more: "In September 1932 Gandhi, imprisoned at Yerawada, fasted unto death against the Communal Award (MacDonald, August 1932), which gave separate electorates to the Depressed Classes. On 24 September 1932 the Poona Pact between Gandhi and Dr B.R. Ambedkar replaced separate electorates with reserved seats (148) in joint electorates. During Quit India Gandhi and Kasturba were interned at the Aga Khan Palace (1942-44), where Kasturba and Mahadev Desai died." } ],
  ['Sevagram (Wardha)',20.736,78.663,'Maharashtra','Gandhi\'s ashram from 1936; Wardha scheme of basic education 1937; Quit India resolution drafted (CWC, July 1942).','gandhian',
    { old: "Segaon (original village name, renamed Sevagram 1940)", more: "Gandhi moved to Segaon near Wardha in 1936 and set up an ashram; the village was renamed Sevagram (\"village of service\") in 1940. The Wardha Scheme of Basic Education (Nai Talim) was formulated here in 1937 under Dr Zakir Husain's committee. The Congress Working Committee met at Wardha on 14 July 1942 and passed the \"Quit India\" resolution, ratified at Bombay on 8 August 1942." } ],
  ['Mahad',18.083,73.417,'Maharashtra (Raigad)','Ambedkar\'s Mahad Satyagraha (Chavdar Tale) Mar 1927; Manusmriti burnt Dec 1927.','other-event',
    { old: "Mahad (Kolaba district, colonial; now Raigad)", more: "On 20 March 1927 Dr B.R. Ambedkar led thousands of Dalits to drink water from the Chavdar Tale (tank) at Mahad, asserting rights under a 1923 Bombay resolution (S.K. Bole) opening public water sources to all. After caste Hindus \"purified\" the tank, Ambedkar held a second conference on 25 December 1927 where the Manusmriti was publicly burnt. 25 December is observed as Manusmriti Dahan Din." } ],
  ['Kakori',26.880,80.800,'Uttar Pradesh (Lucknow)','HRA train action 9 Aug 1925 — Bismil, Ashfaqulla, Rajendra Lahiri, Roshan Singh hanged 1927.','revolutionary-ina',
    { old: "Kakori (Lucknow district); Kakori train action", more: "On 9 August 1925 members of the Hindustan Republican Association (HRA) stopped the 8-Down train at Kakori near Lucknow and looted the government treasury. In the Kakori conspiracy case, Ram Prasad Bismil, Ashfaqulla Khan, Rajendra Lahiri and Thakur Roshan Singh were hanged in December 1927; Chandrashekhar Azad escaped. The UP government renamed it \"Kakori Train Action\" in 2021." } ],
  ['Lahore',31.550,74.344,'Pakistan (Punjab)','Saunders shot Dec 1928 to avenge Lajpat Rai; Lahore Conspiracy Case — Bhagat Singh, Rajguru, Sukhdev hanged 23 Mar 1931.','revolutionary-ina',
    { old: "Lavapura / Lahaur (traditional)", more: "To avenge Lala Lajpat Rai's death after a lathi-charge during the anti-Simon Commission protest (October 1928), Bhagat Singh and Rajguru shot ASP John Saunders on 17 December 1928, mistaking him for J.A. Scott. In the Lahore Conspiracy Case, Bhagat Singh, Sukhdev and Rajguru were hanged in Lahore Central Jail on 23 March 1931 (Shaheed Diwas). Jatin Das died after a 63-day hunger strike in Lahore jail (1929)." } ],
  ['Delhi — Central Legislative Assembly',28.617,77.208,'Delhi','Bhagat Singh & B.K. Dutt threw bombs 8 Apr 1929 against Public Safety & Trade Disputes Bills.','revolutionary-ina',
    { old: "Council House (original name of the building, 1927); Central Legislative Assembly", more: "On 8 April 1929 Bhagat Singh and Batukeshwar Dutt of the Hindustan Socialist Republican Association threw non-lethal bombs and leaflets in the Central Legislative Assembly (in the Council House, now Samvidhan Sadan/old Parliament House) to protest the Public Safety Bill and Trade Disputes Bill, raising \"Inquilab Zindabad\". They courted arrest to use the trial for propaganda; both were transported for life in the Assembly bomb case." } ],
  ['Delhi — Red Fort (INA trials)',28.656,77.241,'Delhi','INA trials Nov 1945 (Shah Nawaz, Sehgal, Dhillon); Bhulabhai Desai defended; nationwide protests.','revolutionary-ina',
    { old: "Qila-i-Mubarak (Mughal name); Shahjahanabad", more: "In November 1945 the first INA trial was held at the Red Fort against Prem Kumar Sahgal (Hindu), Gurbaksh Singh Dhillon (Sikh) and Shah Nawaz Khan (Muslim), symbolising communal unity. Defence counsel included Bhulabhai Desai, Tej Bahadur Sapru, Nehru, Asaf Ali and K.N. Katju. Massive protests forced Commander-in-Chief Auchinleck to remit their transportation sentences (January 1946)." } ],
  ['Prayagraj — Azad Park & Anand Bhavan',25.454,81.848,'Uttar Pradesh','Chandra Shekhar Azad died in Alfred Park 27 Feb 1931; Anand Bhavan, Nehru home, nearby.','revolutionary-ina',
    { old: "Alfred Park / Company Bagh (colonial) → Chandrashekhar Azad Park; Allahabad → Prayagraj (2018)", more: "On 27 February 1931 Chandrashekhar Azad, chief of the HSRA, was surrounded by police in Alfred Park, Allahabad; after a gunfight, he shot himself with his last bullet, keeping his vow never to be captured alive. The park was renamed after him. Nearby Anand Bhavan (Swaraj Bhavan was the original Nehru home, donated to the INC in 1930) was the Nehru family residence and a centre of Congress activity." } ],
  ['Chittagong',22.335,91.833,'Bangladesh','Armoury raid 18 Apr 1930 by Surya Sen (Masterda), Kalpana Dutt, Pritilata Waddedar.','revolutionary-ina',
    { old: "Chattagram (Bengali); Chatgaon; Islamabad (Mughal name, 1666)", more: "On 18 April 1930 the Indian Republican Army led by Surya Sen (\"Masterda\"), with Ganesh Ghosh, Lokenath Bal, Ananta Singh and others, raided the police and auxiliary armouries at Chittagong and cut communications. The revolutionaries fought at Jalalabad Hills (22 April). Pritilata Waddedar led the Pahartali European Club attack (1932) and died; Kalpana Dutt was transported. Surya Sen was hanged in January 1934." } ],
  ['Port Blair — Cellular Jail',11.675,92.748,'Andaman & Nicobar','Kala Pani jail 1906; Savarkar brothers; Netaji hoisted tricolour at Port Blair 30 Dec 1943.','revolutionary-ina',
    { old: "Kala Pani; Port Blair (after Lt Archibald Blair) → Sri Vijaya Puram (2024)", more: "Built between 1896 and 1906 with seven wings of solitary cells, the Cellular Jail held political prisoners including V.D. Savarkar and his brother Ganesh, Barindra Kumar Ghosh, Batukeshwar Dutt and others; hunger strikes (1933, Mahavir Singh died) drew attention. The Japanese occupied the islands in 1942; Subhas Chandra Bose hoisted the tricolour at Port Blair on 30 December 1943. National Memorial since 1979." } ],
  ['Moirang',24.500,93.770,'Manipur (Bishnupur)','INA (Col. Shaukat Malik) hoisted tricolour on Indian mainland, 14 Apr 1944.','revolutionary-ina',
    { old: "Moirang (seat of the ancient Moirang principality; Khamba-Thoibi legend)", more: "During the Japanese-INA advance on Imphal, the INA's Bahadur Group under Colonel Shaukat Hayat Malik hoisted the Indian tricolour at Moirang on 14 April 1944 - the first time the INA flag flew on the Indian mainland. The INA Memorial Complex and war museum stand there. Moirang is also near Loktak Lake." } ],
  ['Imphal',24.807,93.938,'Manipur','Japanese–INA U-Go offensive 1944 defeated (Battle of Imphal, Mar–Jul 1944).','revolutionary-ina',
    { old: "Kangleipak (traditional name of the Manipur kingdom); Imphal", more: "The Japanese Fifteenth Army's U-Go offensive (March-July 1944), joined by INA units (Subhas Brigade, Gandhi and Azad brigades), aimed to capture Imphal and enter India. The British-Indian Fourteenth Army under William Slim, supplied by air, defeated it. With Kohima, it was voted \"Britain's greatest battle\" by the National Army Museum (2013). Heavy losses ended the INA's military campaign." } ],
  ['Kohima',25.666,94.100,'Nagaland','Battle of Kohima Apr–Jun 1944 (tennis court) — Japanese–INA halted; "Stalingrad of the East".','revolutionary-ina',
    { old: "Kewhira (Angami Naga name of Kohima)", more: "From April to June 1944 a small British-Indian garrison held Garrison Hill at Kohima against the Japanese 31st Division, with the fiercest fighting across the Deputy Commissioner's bungalow tennis court. The siege was relieved and the Japanese retreated, ending the threat to India. It is called the \"Stalingrad of the East\". The Kohima War Cemetery bears the epitaph \"When you go home, tell them of us and say...\"." } ],
  ['Mumbai — Gowalia Tank',18.962,72.810,'Maharashtra','Quit India resolution 8 Aug 1942, "Do or Die"; Aruna Asaf Ali hoisted flag 9 Aug — August Kranti Maidan.','gandhian',
    { old: "Gowalia Tank Maidan → August Kranti Maidan; Bombay → Mumbai", more: "At the AICC session at Gowalia Tank, Bombay, the Quit India resolution was passed on 8 August 1942 and Gandhi gave the call \"Do or Die\". The whole Congress leadership was arrested early on 9 August (Operation Zero Hour). Aruna Asaf Ali hoisted the Congress flag at the maidan the same morning. Usha Mehta ran the underground Congress Radio from Bombay." } ],
  ['Ballia',25.760,84.147,'Uttar Pradesh','Parallel government Aug 1942 under Chittu Pandey.','gandhian',
    { old: "Baghi Ballia (\"rebel Ballia\"); Ballia (traditional, after sage Bhrigu/Valmiki - attributed)", more: "During the Quit India movement, on 19 August 1942 people of Ballia freed prisoners from the district jail and set up a parallel national government under Chittu Pandey, who styled himself \"Swaraj Collector\". It lasted only a few days before British troops reoccupied the district with severe repression. Ballia was also the home of Mangal Pandey and later of Chandra Shekhar (PM)." } ],
  ['Tamluk',22.300,87.920,'West Bengal (Purba Medinipur)','Tamralipta Jatiya Sarkar 1942–44; Matangini Hazra shot leading a procession, Sept 1942.','gandhian',
    { old: "Tamralipta / Tamralipti (ancient port, Fa Hien, Hiuen Tsang)", more: "Tamluk was the ancient port of Tamralipta, from where Fa Hien sailed to Sri Lanka. During Quit India, the Tamralipta Jatiya Sarkar (parallel government) functioned from 17 December 1942 to September 1944 in Midnapore, with its own army (Vidyut Vahini) and courts. On 29 September 1942 Matangini Hazra, aged 73, was shot while leading a procession to the Tamluk police station, holding the tricolour." } ],
  ['Satara (Prati Sarkar)',17.688,74.006,'Maharashtra','Parallel government 1943–46 under Nana Patil.','gandhian',
    { old: "Satara (\"seven forts\"); Ajinkyatara", more: "During Quit India, the Prati Sarkar (parallel government) of Satara (1943-46), led by Nana Patil (Krantisinh), Y.B. Chavan and others, set up village people's courts (nyayadan mandals), ran anti-moneylender and anti-liquor drives, and organised a militia (Toofan Sena). It was the longest-lasting parallel government of the movement." } ],
  ['Patna — Secretariat',25.594,85.138,'Bihar','Seven students shot hoisting the flag on the Secretariat, 11 Aug 1942 (Quit India); Martyrs\' Memorial.','gandhian',
    { old: "Pataliputra; Azimabad; Bankipore (colonial civil station)", more: "On 11 August 1942, during Quit India, students tried to hoist the national flag on the Patna Secretariat; police fired and seven students - Umakant Sinha, Ramanand Singh, Satish Prasad Jha, Jagatpati Kumar, Devipada Choudhry, Rajendra Singh and Ramgovind Singh - were killed. The Martyrs' Memorial (Shaheed Smarak) with seven bronze statues by Devi Prasad Roy Chowdhury stands outside the Secretariat." } ],
  ['Gohpur',26.880,93.630,'Assam (Biswanath)','Kanaklata Barua, 17, shot hoisting tricolour at police station, 20 Sept 1942.','gandhian',
    { old: "Gohpur (Darrang district, colonial)", more: "On 20 September 1942, during Quit India, 17-year-old Kanaklata Barua led a procession of the Mrityu Bahini (death squad) to hoist the tricolour at the Gohpur police station; police fired and she was killed along with Mukunda Kakati. She is remembered as \"Birbala\". An Indian Coast Guard fast patrol vessel was named after her (2020)." } ],
  ['Mumbai — HMIS Talwar, Colaba',18.905,72.812,'Maharashtra','Royal Indian Navy mutiny began on HMIS Talwar, 18 Feb 1946; spread to Karachi & other ports.','revolutionary-ina',
    { old: "HMIS Talwar (signals training establishment, Colaba, Bombay)", more: "On 18 February 1946 ratings of HMIS Talwar in Bombay went on strike against poor food, racial discrimination and the INA trials; B.C. Dutt had painted \"Quit India\" slogans. The revolt spread to 78 ships and 20 shore establishments with about 20,000 sailors, including Karachi, and saw sympathy strikes in Bombay. Sardar Patel and Jinnah persuaded the ratings to surrender (23 February). The Cabinet Mission followed." } ],
  ['Shimla — Viceregal Lodge',31.104,77.142,'Himachal Pradesh','Simla Deputation (Aga Khan) 1906; Shimla Conference 1945 on Wavell Plan failed over ML veto.','other-event',
    { old: "Viceregal Lodge (1888) → Rashtrapati Niwas → Indian Institute of Advanced Study (1965); Simla → Shimla", more: "Designed by Henry Irwin and completed in 1888, the Viceregal Lodge was the summer residence of the Viceroy. A Muslim deputation led by the Aga Khan met Lord Minto at Simla on 1 October 1906 demanding separate electorates (Simla Deputation). Lord Wavell's Simla Conference (June-July 1945) failed over the Muslim League's claim to nominate all Muslim members. It now houses the Indian Institute of Advanced Study." } ],
  ['Noakhali',22.833,91.100,'Bangladesh','Gandhi\'s barefoot peace mission after communal riots, Nov 1946–Mar 1947.','gandhian',
    { old: "Noakhali (\"new canal\"); Bhulua (old name of the district)", more: "In October 1946, after Direct Action Day in Calcutta, communal violence against Hindus broke out in Noakhali and Tippera districts of east Bengal. Gandhi arrived in November 1946 and for about four months walked barefoot from village to village (Srirampur, among others) preaching peace and communal harmony, before going to Bihar in March 1947. He later carried out a fast in Calcutta (September 1947)." } ]
 ]
});

